window.DOCKET_SEARCH=[{"t":"Getting started","s":"Start here","u":"index.html","h":[["What Docket is","what-docket-is"],["The pieces","the-pieces"],["Pick your path","pick-your-path"],["Five-minute quick start (Windows)","five-minute-quick-start-windows"],["Next steps","next-steps"]],"x":"Getting started Docket is a free, open-source to-do app in the style of Microsoft To Do. Your tasks live on your own server, on your PC or on a machine you control, and you reach them from Windows, Android, a browser, the command line and AI assistants such as Claude and ChatGPT. # What Docket is Docket is a set of apps on top of Vikunja , an open-source task server. Vikunja stores your tasks; Docket gives you the familiar Microsoft To Do way of working with them: My Day , Important , Planned , lists and groups, subtasks, reminders, repeats and boards. Nobody else holds your data. Docket talks only to the server you sign in to and sends nothing to its developers. # The pieces Piece What it is Runs on Docket for Windows The main app (WinUI 3, Fluent design) Windows 10 and 11 Docket for Android The same features on your phone (.NET MAUI) Android Docket web Vikunja's own web app, reskinned to look like Docket Any browser, at http://localhost:3457 on Windows docket CLI Your tasks from a terminal, with JSON output for scripts Windows docket-mcp connector Lets AI assistants read and change your tasks (it can't delete) Windows, macOS, Linux, Docker Vikunja The task server that stores everything Your PC, a home server or a VPS Every Docket app shares one core library, so My Day, repeats, quick-add words and subtasks behave the same everywhere. # Pick your path Just me, on my Windows PC One installer sets up the server, the app, the CLI and the AI connector. No admin rights needed. Add your phone later with Tailscale . An always-on server One script on any Linux machine with Docker. You get HTTPS, the web app, and a connector that claude.ai and ChatGPT can reach. I already run Vikunja Point the Docket apps and connector at your server. Not sure? Start with the Windows PC. You can move to a server later by backing up and restoring . The setup overview compares the options in more detail. # Five-minute quick start (Windows) Download docket- version -windows-x64.zip from the latest release and unzip it anywhere. Right-click install.ps1 and choose Run with PowerShell . Type your time zone when asked (for example Europe/London or Asia/Kolkata ). Your browser opens. Create your account there, go back to the PowerShell window and press Enter . Sign in once more in the window with the same username and password. Docket makes its own keys (tokens) from that sign-in; your password isn't stored. Answer Y to close sign-ups, so nobody else can make an account. Open Docket from the Start menu and add your first task, for example Send invoice friday !3 . That's it. The Windows guide explains every step and prompt, and what was installed where. Tip Type / in the add bar at the bottom of a list to see the commands: /s search, /x mark done, /m add to My Day, /i star, /ai plan with AI. See quick add and commands . # Next steps Learn the Windows app , the Android app or the CLI . Connect an AI: Claude , ChatGPT or a local model . Then add the Docket skill for Claude so Claude knows how to work with your tasks. Share lists and assign tasks: people, teams and sharing . Something not working? See troubleshooting and the FAQ ."},{"t":"FAQ","s":"Start here","u":"faq.html","h":[["Is Docket free?","is-docket-free"],["Where are my tasks stored?","where-are-my-tasks-stored"],["Do I need a server or a domain name?","do-i-need-a-server-or-a-domain-name"],["Can I use it on my phone?","can-i-use-it-on-my-phone"],["Is there a Mac or Linux app?","is-there-a-mac-or-linux-app"],["Can the AI delete my tasks?","can-the-ai-delete-my-tasks"],["Which AI should I use?","which-ai-should-i-use"],["What's the difference between steps and subtasks?","whats-the-difference-between-steps-and-subtasks"],["Can other people use my Docket?","can-other-people-use-my-docket"],["Can I still use Vikunja's own apps?","can-i-still-use-vikunjas-own-apps"],["How do I update Docket?","how-do-i-update-docket"],["How do I move from my PC to a server?","how-do-i-move-from-my-pc-to-a-server"],["Is Docket made by Microsoft or Vikunja?","is-docket-made-by-microsoft-or-vikunja"]],"x":"Frequently asked questions Short answers to the questions people ask most. For step-by-step fixes, see troubleshooting . # Is Docket free? Yes. Docket is open source under the AGPL-3.0 licence, and so is Vikunja. If you use a cloud AI (Claude, ChatGPT or Gemini) for planning, that provider may charge for its API; local AI with Ollama is free. # Where are my tasks stored? On the Vikunja server you set up. With the Windows installer that's a database in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server on your PC; with Docker it's in Docker volumes on your server. Nothing is stored by Docket's developers, and the apps send no analytics or telemetry. # Do I need a server or a domain name? No. The Windows installer runs the server on your own PC, reachable only from that PC. You need a domain (or a tunnel) only if you want claude.ai or ChatGPT to reach your tasks from the internet. See public address for AI . # Can I use it on my phone? Yes, with the Android app. If your server is on your Windows PC, the easy and private way is Tailscale . There's no iPhone app yet; on iPhone you can use Docket web in the browser. # Is there a Mac or Linux app? Not a native one. On macOS and Linux you can use Docket web in a browser, the AI connector ( docket-mcp ) and the Android app against a Docker server . # Can the AI delete my tasks? No. None of the connector's tools delete anything, and the key (token) it uses has no delete, sharing or team rights either. Deleting stays in the Docket apps, where you can undo it. See how AI works with Docket . # Which AI should I use? Claude or ChatGPT when you want the most capable help, in their apps. A local model (LM Studio, Ollama) when you want everything to stay on your computer. You can connect several at once; they all see the same tasks. The AI guide has a table. # What's the difference between steps and subtasks? Steps are a simple checklist inside one task (like Microsoft To Do). Subtasks are full tasks of their own, with their own dates, star, reminders and subtasks, nested as deep as you like; a task can even sit under several parents. Turn old steps into subtasks with one click, or docket convert id . See the Windows app . # Can other people use my Docket? Yes. Add people on the People page, share a list with them or with a team, and assign tasks. See people, teams and sharing . # Can I still use Vikunja's own apps? Yes. Docket stores everything in plain Vikunja, so Vikunja's web app (or Docket web , its reskin) and API see the same tasks. A few Docket repeats (weekdays, yearly, every N months) only advance when you complete the task in a Docket app. Details in the developer API . # How do I update Docket? Run the new release's install.ps1 on Windows, or docker compose pull docker compose up -d on a server. See updating . # How do I move from my PC to a server? Make a backup on the PC, set up the Docker server , then restore the backup there. See backups and restore . # Is Docket made by Microsoft or Vikunja? No. Docket is an independent project by Quartifex. It isn't affiliated with Vikunja, Microsoft, Anthropic or OpenAI."},{"t":"Choose a setup","s":"Set up","u":"self-hosting.html","h":[["Windows PC","windows-pc"],["If the server stops","if-the-server-stops"],["Reach it from your phone","reach-it-from-your-phone"],["Docker server","docker-server"],["No public IP or domain","no-public-ip-or-domain"],["Your own Vikunja","your-own-vikunja"],["Docket web skin","docket-web-skin"],["Looking after it","looking-after-it"]],"x":"Choose a setup Docket keeps your tasks in Vikunja , an open-source task server, and adds the Docket apps and an AI connector on top. You can run it three ways. All three give you the same apps and the same features. Windows PC Docker server Your own Vikunja Best for Just you, on one PC (phone through Tailscale) Always on: a VPS, NAS or home server You already run Vikunja You get Vikunja on 127.0.0.1, the app, CLI, Docket web and the local AI connector Vikunja, Docket web, the remote AI connector and automatic HTTPS The Docket apps and connector claude.ai and ChatGPT Only through a tunnel Yes Run the connector yourself Skill needed Run one script Basic Linux and Docker Reverse proxy setup Guide Windows PC Docker server Existing Vikunja Claude Desktop, Claude Code, local models and the Docket apps work with every setup. # Windows PC Download the Windows zip from Releases , right-click install.ps1 and choose Run with PowerShell . It installs everything for your Windows account, without admin rights. Full guide, including what each question means: Windows PC . # If the server stops The apps, the CLI and the AI connector restart a stopped server on your PC by themselves, and Windows checks it every 5 minutes as a failsafe. Details: if the server stops . # Reach it from your phone The Windows install listens only on your PC. Use Tailscale to reach it privately from your phone: tailscale serve --bg 3457 on the PC, then sign in to Docket for Android with the address it gives you. # Docker server One script on any Linux machine with Docker sets up Vikunja, Docket web, the AI connector and HTTPS certificates. Full guide: Docker server . # No public IP or domain claude.ai and ChatGPT need a public HTTPS address for the connector. Without opening ports, use Tailscale Funnel or a Cloudflare Tunnel: public address for AI . # Your own Vikunja Docket works with any Vikunja 2.x server: existing Vikunja . # Docket web skin docket-web serves Vikunja's web app with the Docket look. It's included in the Windows and Docker setups: Docket web . # Looking after it Updating Docket and Vikunja Backups and restore Uninstalling Security"},{"t":"Windows PC","s":"Set up","u":"setup/windows.html","h":[["Step by step","step-by-step"],["What each question means","what-each-question-means"],["What gets installed where","what-gets-installed-where"],["Options","options"],["If the server stops","if-the-server-stops"],["Connect it to your account again","connect-it-to-your-account-again"],["From source","from-source"]],"x":"Install on a Windows PC The Windows installer sets up everything on one PC: the Vikunja task server, the Docket app, the docket command, Docket web and the AI connector. It takes about five minutes and needs no admin rights. Everything is installed for your Windows account only. You need Windows 10 or 11 (x64 or Arm64) and an internet connection for the download. # Step by step Download. Get docket- version -windows-x64.zip (or -arm64.zip for Arm PCs such as Surface Pro X or Snapdragon laptops) from the latest release . Unzip it into any folder, for example your Downloads folder. (Don't run it from inside the zip.) Run the installer. Right-click install.ps1 and choose Run with PowerShell . If PowerShell asks about the execution policy, or the window closes straight away, open PowerShell in that folder (in File Explorer: File Open Windows PowerShell , or type powershell in the address bar) and run: powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1 Answer the questions (explained below). Your browser opens Vikunja: create your account there. Open Docket from the Start menu. # What each question means The installer prints what it's doing in sections ( == Vikunja , == Scheduled tasks , == Your account …). It asks only a few things: The installer asks What it means What to answer Your time zone, e.g. Europe/London, America/New_York, Asia/Kolkata Used for today , due dates and reminders Your time zone name , like Europe/London Press Enter when your account exists Your browser has opened Vikunja's sign-up page at http://localhost:3456/register Create an account (username, email, password), then press Enter Vikunja username and Vikunja password One sign-in, so the installer can make keys (API tokens) for the app and for AI The account you just created. The password isn't saved Two-factor code (press Enter if you have not turned it on) Only if you've turned on two-factor sign-in in Vikunja Press Enter on a fresh install Close sign-ups so nobody else can make an account on this server? [Y/n] Stops new accounts being created on your server Y (you can still add people from the Docket app) Docket is already connected to an account. Connect again (new tokens)? [y/N] Only when you run the installer again N to keep things as they are; Y to make fresh tokens After signing in, the installer: makes a token for the Docket app and CLI, and a separate no-delete token for the AI connector; adds Docket to Claude Desktop and Claude Code if you have them (quit and reopen Claude Desktop to load it); prints where everything is, and how to uninstall. Note Windows may show Windows protected your PC for the downloaded script or app. Choose More info Run anyway . The release builds aren't code-signed yet. # What gets installed where What Where Docket app, docket CLI, docket-mcp , Docket web %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Docket (the CLI is added to your PATH; Start menu shortcut Docket ) Vikunja and your data %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server (kept outside OneDrive: a live database and file sync don't mix) Backups Documents\\Docket\\backups , every night at 11 pm, newest 14 kept Settings and tokens %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\settings.json (tokens encrypted with Windows DPAPI for your account) Server log %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server\\logs Scheduled tasks (Task Scheduler Task Scheduler Library Docket ): Task What it does Vikunja server Starts the task server when you sign in, then checks every 5 minutes and restarts it if it stopped Docket web Runs Docket web at http://localhost:3457 , with the same 5-minute check Vikunja backup Nightly backup at 11 pm Docket reminders Only with -Reminders : shows reminder notifications from sign-in The server listens on 127.0.0.1 only, so other devices on your network can't reach it. To use your phone, see phone access . # Options Add these after install.ps1 when you run it from PowerShell: Option What it does -Reminders Shows reminder notifications from sign-in (adds the Docket reminders task). Recommended -TimeZone Europe/London Skips the time-zone question -Port 3500 Runs Vikunja on another port (default 3456) -NoScheduledTasks -NoPath -NoStartMenu A portable setup: no scheduled tasks, PATH entry or shortcut -SkipAccountSetup Installs the programs but doesn't ask you to sign in For example: powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1 -Reminders -TimeZone Asia/Kolkata Run the installer again at any time to upgrade or repair; your tasks stay. See updating . # If the server stops The task server keeps itself running, with three layers of protection: The apps restart it. When the Docket app, the docket CLI or the AI connector finds the server on this PC stopped, it starts it again (through its scheduled task, or the server program directly), waits for it, and then carries on with what you asked. You see Starting your task server… for a few seconds instead of an error. The scheduled tasks check every 5 minutes. Vikunja server , Docket web and Docket reminders run at sign-in and again every 5 minutes. A running copy is left alone; a stopped one starts again. The reminders watcher heals it too. docket notify --watch runs all day; if the server stops, it restarts it and keeps watching. If the server still can't start, the app says so and offers Start server and retry . If that fails too, restart the PC, or run install.ps1 again (it keeps your data). The server's log is in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server\\logs . A server on another machine (Docker, a VPS) can't be started from your PC: there the app tells you it can't reach it. # Connect it to your account again If tokens stop working (they last a year), or the app says a token can't do something, make new ones: $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\setup-tokens.ps1 It asks for your Vikunja username and password once, replaces the app's and the AI connector's tokens, and reconnects Claude Desktop and Claude Code. For a server elsewhere, add -ServerUrl https://your-server . # From source To build from the repository instead of a release: install the .NET 10 S"},{"t":"Phone access (Tailscale)","s":"Set up","u":"setup/phone.html","h":[["Set it up","set-it-up"],["Checking it works","checking-it-works"],["Local AI on the phone","local-ai-on-the-phone"],["Letting claude.ai or ChatGPT in","letting-claudeai-or-chatgpt-in"]],"x":"Phone access with Tailscale The Windows install keeps your task server private: it listens only on your PC ( 127.0.0.1 ). To reach it from your phone without opening anything to the internet, use Tailscale . It's free for personal use and makes a private network of your own devices. Note Using a Docker server with a domain? You don't need this page: sign in on the phone with https://your-domain . # Set it up Install Tailscale on the PC from tailscale.com/download and sign in (tray icon Log in ). Install Tailscale on the phone (Google Play) and sign in to the same account . Share Docket from the PC. Open PowerShell and run: tailscale serve --bg 3457 This gives your PC an HTTPS address like https://my-pc.tail1234.ts.net that only your own devices can open. Port 3457 is Docket web , so the same address also works in your phone's browser. (Use 3456 to share plain Vikunja instead; the Docket apps work with either.) Install Docket for Android (the APK from Releases , or the Play Store when it's listed) and open it. Sign in with the address from step 3 and your Vikunja username and password. Docket creates a token for the phone; your password isn't stored. Run tailscale serve status on the PC to see the address again. The PC has to be on (and signed in) for the phone to reach it; the phone keeps working offline only for what's already on screen. # Checking it works On the phone, with Tailscale connected, open the address in the browser. You should see Docket web (or Vikunja). If it doesn't load, check that both devices show as connected in the Tailscale app, and that the PC's server is running (open Docket on the PC). # Local AI on the phone Docket for Android can plan tasks with Ollama running on your PC. Ollama has to accept connections from other devices for that: see AI planning . # Letting claude.ai or ChatGPT in Tailscale serve is private to your devices, which is what you want for the apps. Web assistants connect from the internet, so they need a public address for the AI connector only: see public address for AI ."},{"t":"Docker server","s":"Set up","u":"setup/docker.html","h":[["What you need","what-you-need"],["Set it up","set-it-up"],["What setup.sh asks","what-setupsh-asks"],["By hand instead","by-hand-instead"],["Addresses","addresses"],["Settings in .env","settings-in-env"],["Looking after it","looking-after-it"],["How it fits together","how-it-fits-together"]],"x":"Run a Docker server An always-on Docket server, for a VPS, a NAS or a home server. One script sets up: Vikunja , the task server; Docket web , Vikunja's web app with the Docket look, at https://your-domain/ ; the AI connector , so claude.ai, ChatGPT and other assistants can reach your tasks, at https://your-domain/mcp ; Caddy , which gets and renews HTTPS certificates automatically. # What you need A Linux machine with Docker and the compose plugin. Any VPS with 1 GB of memory is plenty. A domain or subdomain (for example tasks.example.com ) whose DNS points at the machine. Ports 80 and 443 open to the internet (Caddy needs them for certificates). No domain, or behind a home router you can't open? See public address for AI , or use Tailscale if you only need your own devices. # Set it up From a release (uses the published connector image): curl -fsSL -o docket-server.tar.gz https://github.com/niteshaggarwal/docket/releases/latest/download/docket-server- version .tar.gz tar xzf docket-server.tar.gz cd docket-server ./setup.sh Or from a clone of the repository (builds the connector image locally if it can't be pulled): git clone https://github.com/niteshaggarwal/docket cd docket/deploy/docker ./setup.sh # What setup.sh asks Question What to answer Domain for this server (DNS must point here, ports 80+443 open) Your domain, e.g. tasks.example.com Time zone [Europe/London] Press Enter to accept, or type yours (e.g. Asia/Kolkata ) 1. Open https://your-domain and create your account… Press Enter Open the address, create your account, then press Enter . The first certificate can take a minute 2. Sign in once so the AI connector gets its own no-delete token Your Vikunja username and password. The connector saves a token without delete rights; the password isn't stored 3. Close sign-ups so nobody else can make an account? [Y/n] Y . You can still add people from the Docket app setup.sh writes the settings to .env with fresh random secrets (readable only by you) and starts the stack. At the end it prints your addresses and where the connector key is: DOCKET_MCP_KEY in .env . You'll type that key once when you connect claude.ai or ChatGPT. # By hand instead cp .env.example .env # fill in DOCKET_DOMAIN, TZ, VIKUNJA_SECRET and DOCKET_MCP_KEY chmod 600 .env docker compose up -d # then create your account at https://your-domain docker compose exec connector docket-mcp setup # close sign-ups: set VIKUNJA_REGISTRATION=false in .env, then docker compose up -d Make the two secrets with openssl rand -hex 32 . # Addresses Address What https://your-domain/ Docket web . Sign in here from the Docket apps too https://your-domain/mcp MCP for AI assistants ( AI guides ) https://your-domain/openapi.json REST description for GPT actions and automations ( API ) https://your-domain/connector/health Connector status; should say status : ok Then: Windows app: in Docket's Settings Connection, enter https://your-domain and a token, or run setup-tokens.ps1 -ServerUrl https://your-domain ( how ). Android: sign in with https://your-domain . AI: Claude , ChatGPT , others . # Settings in .env Setting Meaning DOCKET_DOMAIN The public address of this server TZ Your time zone, used for due dates and reminders VIKUNJA_SECRET Secret for Vikunja's sign-in tokens DOCKET_MCP_KEY The connector key (24+ characters). Changing it signs out every AI VIKUNJA_REGISTRATION true while you create accounts, then false DOCKET_DEFAULT_LIST Optional: list id that new AI tasks go to when no list is named DOCKET_VERSION , VIKUNJA_VERSION Optional: pin image versions HTTP_PORT , HTTPS_PORT Optional: other ports for Caddy After changing .env , run docker compose up -d . # Looking after it Update: docker compose pull docker compose up -d . See updating . Back up: see backups and restore . Logs: docker compose logs -f connector (or vikunja , web , caddy ). Security: see security . Keep .env private; it holds your secrets. # How it fits together Caddy is the only service open to the internet. It sends the connector's routes ( /mcp , /oauth/* , /.well-known/oauth-* , /api/tools/* , /openapi.json , and /connector/health as /health ) to the connector, and everything else to Docket web, which passes requests on to Vikunja. If web isn't running, Caddy falls back to Vikunja directly. The data lives in Docker volumes: vikunja-db (the database), vikunja-files (attachments and backups) and connector-data (the connector's token and OAuth sign-ins)."},{"t":"Public address for AI","s":"Set up","u":"setup/public-address.html","h":[["Tailscale Funnel","tailscale-funnel"],["Cloudflare Tunnel","cloudflare-tunnel"],["Check it from outside","check-it-from-outside"]],"x":"Public address for AI claude.ai and ChatGPT run in the cloud, so they connect to your tasks from the internet. That means the AI connector needs a public HTTPS address. The Docket apps, Claude Desktop, Claude Code and local models don't need this; they work privately. You have three options: Option Good for Opens ports? A Docker server with a domain Always-on servers Yes, 80 and 443 Tailscale Funnel The Windows install No Cloudflare Tunnel A Docker server at home No Whichever you pick, only the connector becomes public, and it's protected by your connector key. Pick a long random key and keep it private. # Tailscale Funnel Funnel publishes one port of your PC on a public ts.net address with an HTTPS certificate. Use it to let claude.ai or ChatGPT reach a Windows install. Set up Tailscale on the PC, and turn on Funnel for your tailnet in the Tailscale admin console (Access controls; Tailscale's Funnel guide has the current steps). Run the connector over HTTP on the PC, with a key and your public address: $env:DOCKET_MCP_KEY = a-long-random-key-of-24-or-more-characters $env:DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL = https://my-pc.tail1234.ts.net $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\mcp\\docket-mcp.exe --http It listens on http://127.0.0.1:8080 and reads your Docket settings for the server and token. In another PowerShell window, publish it: tailscale funnel --bg 8080 Connect Claude or ChatGPT to https://my-pc.tail1234.ts.net/mcp , and type the key when asked. Vikunja itself stays private. The connector needs to keep running: to start it at sign-in, add it to Task Scheduler or a startup script with the same environment variables. Warning Funnel and serve share ports on the PC. If you use tailscale serve for your phone on 3457, keep Funnel on a different port (8080 above). # Cloudflare Tunnel Good for a Docker server at home. Run cloudflared with a hostname on your Cloudflare domain, and set DOCKET_DOMAIN and DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL to that hostname. Cloudflare handles HTTPS, so leave Caddy out and point the tunnel at the services directly: Path Send to /mcp , /oauth/* , /.well-known/oauth-* , /api/tools/* , /openapi.json connector:8080 everything else web:8080 ( Docket web ) See Cloudflare's tunnel documentation for creating the tunnel. # Check it from outside From a phone on mobile data (not your Wi-Fi), open the connector's health page: https://your-address/health when the connector is published directly (Funnel), or https://your-domain/connector/health on the Docker server. It should say status : ok . If it doesn't load there, claude.ai and ChatGPT can't reach it either."},{"t":"Existing Vikunja","s":"Set up","u":"setup/existing-vikunja.html","h":[["Android app","android-app"],["Windows app and CLI","windows-app-and-cli"],["AI connector","ai-connector"],["Docket web","docket-web"],["Good to know","good-to-know"]],"x":"Use your own Vikunja Already run Vikunja ? Docket works with any Vikunja 2.x server. Your existing projects become Docket lists, and nothing about your server has to change. # Android app Sign in with your server's address (for example https://tasks.example.com ) and your username and password. Docket makes a token for the phone; the password isn't stored. # Windows app and CLI Install Docket with the Windows installer , then connect it to your server instead of a local one: $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\setup-tokens.ps1 -ServerUrl https://your-vikunja It signs in once and makes two tokens: one for the app and CLI, and a no-delete one for the AI connector. You can also enter the address and an API token by hand in Settings Connection , or with docket login --server https://your-vikunja . If you don't want a local server at all, run the installer with -NoScheduledTasks -SkipAccountSetup , then run setup-tokens.ps1 as above. # AI connector The connector needs your server's address and a token. docket-mcp setup makes a no-delete token for you: export DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL=https://your-vikunja export DOCKET_DATA_DIR=~/.config/docket docket-mcp setup # asks for your username and password once, saves the token docket-mcp # local (stdio), for Claude Desktop, Claude Code, LM Studio… docket-mcp --http # remote (HTTP), for claude.ai and ChatGPT behind your HTTPS proxy For remote use, set DOCKET_MCP_KEY (24+ characters) and DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL (your public HTTPS address) too, or run the container image instead: docker run -d --name docket-connector -p 127.0.0.1:8080:8080 \\ -e DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL=https://your-vikunja -e DOCKET_MCP_KEY=... -e DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL=https://your-vikunja \\ -v docket-connector:/data ghcr.io/niteshaggarwal/docket-connector docker exec -it docket-connector docket-mcp setup Then route these paths from your reverse proxy to the connector, and everything else to Vikunja (or Docket web): /mcp , /oauth/* , /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource* , /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server* , /api/tools/* and /openapi.json . deploy/docker/Caddyfile is a working example. All connector settings are listed in how AI works with Docket . # Docket web To give your Vikunja the Docket look, run docket-web in front of it: see Docket web . # Good to know Docket stores its features in plain Vikunja: My Day is a label, Important is a favourite, steps are a checklist in the description. See how Docket maps onto Vikunja . Avoid renaming the My Day label or the labels that start with ↻ ; Docket uses them."},{"t":"Docket web","s":"Set up","u":"setup/docket-web.html","h":[["Where it runs","where-it-runs"],["Windows","windows"],["Docker","docker"],["Your own Vikunja","your-own-vikunja"],["Settings","settings"],["How it works","how-it-works"]],"x":"Docket web docket-web is Vikunja's own web app dressed as Docket: the Fluent look of the Windows app in light and dark, with Docket's name and icons instead of Vikunja's. Every Vikunja feature stays: list, Gantt, table and Kanban views, upcoming, teams, labels, sharing and settings. Use it on a Mac, a Linux PC, an iPhone or any computer with a browser. How to use it day to day: the web app . This page is about running it. # Where it runs Setup Address Started by Windows PC http://localhost:3457 Scheduled task Docket web , at sign-in and every 5 minutes Docker server https://your-domain/ The web service in compose.yaml Your own Vikunja Wherever you route it You On Windows, the app's Open in browser goes to Docket web when it's running, otherwise to Vikunja. # Windows install.ps1 installs it to `%LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Docket\\web` and starts it at sign-in. To run it by hand: $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\web\\docket-web.exe It forwards to Vikunja on port 3456 and listens on http://127.0.0.1:3457 . To open it on your phone through Tailscale, run tailscale serve --bg 3457 ( phone access ). The Docket apps can sign in through either port. # Docker Included. The web service (the connector image, run as /app/web/docket-web ) serves the site's root, and Caddy sends everything except the connector's routes to it. If web isn't running, Caddy falls back to Vikunja directly. It needs a connector image that includes docket-web (0.3.0 or newer). If you pin DOCKET_VERSION to an older one, remove the web service or update the pin. # Your own Vikunja Run docket-web with DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL set and route your site's root to it, keeping the connector's routes ( /mcp , /oauth/* , /.well-known/oauth-* , /api/tools/* , /openapi.json ) going to the connector. # Settings Variable Default What DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL http://127.0.0.1:3456 The Vikunja server to forward to DOCKET_WEB_URLS http://127.0.0.1:3457 Address(es) to listen on, separated by ; DOCKET_WEB_SKIN_DIR (none) Serve the skin from this folder instead of the built-in copy (for working on the skin) # How it works It's a small reverse proxy in front of Vikunja. Requests, the WebSocket, uploads and downloads, and public /share/... links pass straight through; only the HTML page and the web manifest are rewritten to add the skin ( /docket-skin/skin.css and skin.js , built into the program). Signing in, the API and the Docket apps work the same through it, since it only changes how pages look. The page reaches the API at whatever address you opened it on, so Vikunja's service.publicurl can stay as it is."},{"t":"Updating","s":"Set up","u":"setup/updating.html","h":[["Windows","windows"],["Docker server","docker-server"],["Android","android"],["AI connector on macOS or Linux","ai-connector-on-macos-or-linux"]],"x":"Updating New Docket versions are listed on Releases , with what changed in the changelog . Your tasks are kept when you update. # Windows Download the new docket- version -windows-x64.zip and unzip it. Close the Docket app. Run the new install.ps1 the same way as the first time (right-click Run with PowerShell ). When it asks Docket is already connected to an account. Connect again (new tokens)? , answer N . The installer replaces the programs, keeps your data and settings, and updates Vikunja to the latest release. To pin Vikunja to a version, add -VikunjaVersion v2.7.1 (any tag from Vikunja's releases ). Tip A backup is made every night, but before a big update you can make one straight away: Task Scheduler Docket Vikunja backup Run. See backups . # Docker server In the folder with compose.yaml : docker compose pull docker compose up -d To stay on known versions, set DOCKET_VERSION (the connector and Docket web) and VIKUNJA_VERSION in .env , then run the same command after changing them. Read Vikunja's release notes before a major-version jump, and make a backup first. After upgrading the connector, ChatGPT may not see new tools until you remove and add the connector again ( why ). # Android Install the new APK from Releases over the old one (or update from the Play Store once Docket is listed there). You stay signed in. # AI connector on macOS or Linux Download the new docket-mcp for your platform from Releases and replace the old file. Restart the AI app that uses it."},{"t":"Backups and restore","s":"Set up","u":"setup/backups.html","h":[["Windows","windows"],["Restore on Windows","restore-on-windows"],["Docker server","docker-server"],["Restore on Docker","restore-on-docker"],["Moving from your PC to a server","moving-from-your-pc-to-a-server"]],"x":"Backups and restore All your tasks, lists, files and people live in Vikunja, so backing up Vikunja backs up Docket. Vikunja's dump command makes one zip with the database, your attachments and its settings. # Windows The installer already backs up for you: When: every night at 11 pm (scheduled task Vikunja backup ). If the PC is off, it runs at the next chance. Where: Documents\\Docket\\backups , files named vikunja-dump_ date .zip . The newest 14 are kept. Make one now: Task Scheduler Task Scheduler Library Docket Vikunja backup Run . Documents is often backed up by OneDrive, which gives you an off-PC copy. The live database itself is kept outside OneDrive on purpose. # Restore on Windows Quit the Docket app. Stop the server: Task Scheduler Docket right-click Vikunja server End , and also Disable it for now so the 5-minute check doesn't start it again while you restore. In PowerShell, run (with the backup you want): $server = $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Docket\\server $server\\bin\\vikunja.exe --config $server\\config.yml restore $env:USERPROFILE\\Documents\\Docket\\backups\\vikunja-dump_XXXX.zip Enable the Vikunja server task again and Run it, then open Docket. Warning Restoring replaces everything on the server with the backup. Make a fresh backup first if you might want today's tasks back. # Docker server Make a backup inside the container, then copy it out: docker compose exec vikunja /app/vikunja/vikunja dump --path /app/vikunja/files/backups docker compose cp vikunja:/app/vikunja/files/backups ./backups Run this from cron (for example nightly) and copy ./backups somewhere off the server. # Restore on Docker Copy the zip into the container and run restore , then restart Vikunja: docker compose cp ./backups/vikunja-dump_XXXX.zip vikunja:/app/vikunja/files/restore.zip docker compose exec vikunja /app/vikunja/vikunja restore /app/vikunja/files/restore.zip docker compose restart vikunja The connector's volume holds only its token and OAuth sign-ins. You don't need to back it up: run docker compose exec connector docket-mcp setup again and reconnect your AI apps. # Moving from your PC to a server On the PC, make a backup now (see above). Set up the Docker server and create an account. Restore the PC's backup on the server (above). Your account from the PC comes along with it. Point the apps at the server: Android, sign in with https://your-domain ; Windows, run setup-tokens.ps1 -ServerUrl https://your-domain . Vikunja's backup documentation has more detail, including database-specific options."},{"t":"Uninstalling","s":"Set up","u":"setup/uninstalling.html","h":[["Windows","windows"],["Docker server","docker-server"],["Android","android"],["AI connections","ai-connections"]],"x":"Uninstalling # Windows Run the uninstaller from PowerShell: powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\uninstall.ps1 It removes the scheduled tasks, the programs, the PATH entry, the Start menu shortcut and the Claude Code connector. Your tasks are kept in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server , so installing again brings everything back. To delete your tasks too, add -RemoveData : powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\uninstall.ps1 -RemoveData That deletes the server data and Docket's settings. Backups in Documents\\Docket\\backups are always kept; delete that folder yourself if you want them gone. Afterwards: If you added Docket to Claude Desktop , LM Studio or another AI app, remove the docket entry from its MCP settings (Claude Desktop: Settings Developer Edit Config). If you used Tailscale for your phone, run tailscale serve reset on the PC. # Docker server In the folder with compose.yaml : docker compose down # stops the stack, keeps your data in Docker volumes docker compose down -v # stops it and deletes all data (tasks, files, certificates) Make a backup first if you might want your tasks later. # Android Uninstall the app as usual. Its token stays valid on the server until you delete it in Vikunja (Settings API Tokens), or uninstall the server. # AI connections claude.ai / ChatGPT: remove the Docket connector in their settings. All AI at once (server): change DOCKET_MCP_KEY in .env and run docker compose up -d . The connector's token: delete Docket AI connector in Vikunja under Settings API Tokens."},{"t":"Security","s":"Set up","u":"setup/security.html","h":[["How Docket protects your data","how-docket-protects-your-data"],["The remote connector","the-remote-connector"],["Checklist for servers","checklist-for-servers"],["Revoking access","revoking-access"],["Sharing and public links","sharing-and-public-links"]],"x":"Security Docket is built so your tasks stay yours, and so an AI can help without being able to do damage. This page explains what protects your data and how to keep a server safe. To report a security problem, see SECURITY.md (please report privately, not as a public issue). # How Docket protects your data Your server, your data. Docket talks only to the Vikunja server you sign in to. It sends nothing to the developers: no analytics, no telemetry. Tokens, not passwords. Setup signs in once and creates API tokens; passwords are never stored. On Windows, tokens are encrypted with DPAPI for your Windows account. On Android they're in the system's secure storage. Least privilege for AI. The AI connector gets its own token with no delete, sharing, team, webhook, migration or CalDAV rights, and none of its tools delete. You can revoke it in Vikunja under Settings API Tokens. Private by default on Windows. The Windows install listens on 127.0.0.1 only. Nothing on your network can reach it until you choose to share it (for example with Tailscale). HTTPS on servers. The Docker stack exposes only Caddy, with automatic certificates and HSTS. # The remote connector When the connector is reachable from the internet (for claude.ai and ChatGPT): Every request to /mcp and /api/tools/* needs the connector key or an OAuth token made with it. OAuth tokens are stored only as SHA-256 hashes, and changing the key revokes them all. OAuth sign-ins only return to the Claude and ChatGPT sites (and localhost) unless you add hosts with DOCKET_OAUTH_REDIRECT_HOSTS , so a fake client can't collect your key. The sign-in page shows where access will go and can't be framed. Only approve a connection you started yourself. Ten wrong keys from one address in 15 minutes pause sign-in. # Checklist for servers Use a long random DOCKET_MCP_KEY ( openssl rand -hex 32 ; setup.sh does this) and keep .env readable only by you ( chmod 600 .env ). Close Vikunja sign-ups after creating your accounts ( VIKUNJA_REGISTRATION=false ). Turn on two-factor sign-in in Vikunja's settings. Keep images up to date: docker compose pull docker compose up -d . Prefer private access (Tailscale) when you don't need web assistants like claude.ai or ChatGPT. Back up regularly ( how ). # Revoking access To cut off Do this Every AI connected over the internet Change DOCKET_MCP_KEY and restart the connector ( docker compose up -d ) One AI app Remove the Docket connector in that app The AI connector's token Delete Docket AI connector in Vikunja, Settings API Tokens A lost phone Delete that phone's token in Vikunja, Settings API Tokens, and change your password # Sharing and public links A public link lets anyone with the link see the list, so add a password if it holds anything private. Remove links you no longer need from the list's Share options. See people, teams and sharing ."},{"t":"Overview","s":"Use Docket","u":"apps.html","h":[["Windows app","windows-app"],["Android app","android-app"],["Web app","web-app"],["docket command line (Windows)","docket-command-line-windows"],["More","more"]],"x":"Using Docket Docket comes as a Windows app, an Android app, a web app and a command line. They all show the same tasks and behave the same way, because they share one core library: My Day, repeats, quick-add words and subtasks work identically everywhere. Windows app Android app Docket web docket CLI My Day, Important, Planned, All, Completed ✓ ✓ Vikunja's views ✓ Subtasks, steps, reminders, repeats ✓ ✓ ✓ (Vikunja's) ✓ Overview, Upcoming, Open tasks ✓ ✓ Upcoming ✓ List, Table, Gantt and Board views ✓ ✓ (timeline for Gantt) ✓ Board People, teams and sharing ✓ ✓ (new accounts from the PC) ✓ ✓ Add-bar commands and AI planning ✓ ✓ docket ai Reminder notifications ✓ (with docket notify --watch ) ✓ ✓ # Windows app Start menu Docket . Smart lists, lists and groups, the details pane, boards, people, AI planning and more: the Windows app . Also see keyboard shortcuts and quick add and commands . # Android app Sign in with your server's address (for a Windows install, use Tailscale ). Swipe right to add to My Day, left to delete: the Android app . # Web app Open http://localhost:3457 on the PC with Docket, or your server's address: Docket web . # docket command line (Windows) docket shows today's tasks; docket add Send invoice friday !3 adds one. Every command, with examples and JSON output for scripts: the command line . # More Views, boards and list styles People, teams and sharing AI planning in the app"},{"t":"Windows app","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/windows.html","h":[["The sidebar","the-sidebar"],["Lists and groups","lists-and-groups"],["Adding tasks","adding-tasks"],["Task details","task-details"],["Subtasks and steps","subtasks-and-steps"],["Views and boards","views-and-boards"],["People, teams and sharing","people-teams-and-sharing"],["AI planning","ai-planning"],["Reminders","reminders"],["Settings","settings"],["Open in browser","open-in-browser"],["If the server isn't running","if-the-server-isnt-running"],["Repeats that only Docket understands","repeats-that-only-docket-understands"]],"x":"The Windows app Docket for Windows works like Microsoft To Do, with subtasks, boards, people and AI added. Open it from the Start menu ( Docket ). If you haven't installed it yet, see install on a Windows PC . # The sidebar The left side has your smart lists at the top and your lists underneath. Smart list What's in it My Day Tasks you picked for today. It starts empty each morning. Suggestions offers overdue, due and recently added tasks Important Starred tasks Planned Tasks with a due date, grouped Earlier, Today, Tomorrow, This week and Later All Every open task Completed Finished tasks Tasks Your default list (Inbox) Overview A dashboard: counts, the last 14 days, priorities, today, overdue, upcoming, assigned to you and progress per list Upcoming What's due soon, day by day Open tasks Everything unfinished, across all lists Hide the ones you don't use in Settings Smart lists (there's also Hide empty smart lists automatically ). # Lists and groups New list: New list at the bottom of the sidebar, or Ctrl + L . New group makes a group. Groups hold several lists (in Vikunja they're parent projects). List options (the … button at the top of a list) has Rename list , Colour , Move to group , Icon… , Background… , Share list… , Show completed tasks , Copy list and Delete list . Drag a task onto a list in the sidebar to move it there. Drag tasks up and down to reorder them when the list is sorted by My order . Lists can look the way you like, with an icon and a background: see views, boards and list styles . # Adding tasks The add bar sits at the bottom of every list. Type and press Enter . Quick-add words set the due date and priority as you type: Send invoice friday !3 Call the bank tomorrow Renew passport in 3 days !5 Type / for commands: /s search, /x mark matches done, /m add them to My Day, /i star them, /ai plan with AI. Everything about the add bar: quick add and commands . # Task details Click a task (or press Enter ) to open the details pane on the right. There you can set: Subtasks ( Add subtask ) and Parents ( Add parent… ); see below Checklist : the older steps, with Turn into subtasks Add to My Day and Important (the star) Set priority : none, low, medium, high, urgent or do now Add start date , Add end date , Add due date and Remind me Repeat : daily, weekdays, weekly, monthly, yearly or custom (every N days, weeks, months or years) Assign to : people who can see the list ( people and sharing ) Categories : coloured labels (type a new one and press Enter ) Add file : attach and open files List : move the task to another list Add note Activity : who created it and when, when it last changed and when it was completed Delete task is at the bottom of the pane, with Undo for a few seconds afterwards. (Settings General Confirm before deleting asks first.) Esc hides the pane. Small icons on each task row show what it has: notes, files, reminders, repeats, subtask progress, priority and assignees. # Subtasks and steps Subtasks are full tasks of their own: each has its own star, dates, reminders and subtasks, and they nest as deep as you need. A task can even sit under several parents (for example Book venue under both Launch party and Q4 budget ). Add one in the details pane under Subtasks ( Add subtask ; quick-add words work), or with the CLI: docket sub 12 Book the venue friday . Put an existing task under another with Parents Add parent… , or docket parent 15 12 . Expand or collapse subtasks with the arrow next to a task, or → and ← . Steps (the Checklist ) are the simple list inside a task, like Microsoft To Do's. Turn them into subtasks with Turn into subtasks in the details pane, or docket convert id . # Views and boards Each list can be shown as a List , Table , Gantt chart or Board , with the view buttons at the top of the list. Boards have columns you can name, colour and give an icon. See views, boards and list styles . At the top of a list you can also sort (my order, importance, due date, A–Z or creation date), show or hide completed tasks , search , and copy the list as text . # People, teams and sharing The People and teams page (in the sidebar) lists everyone you work with. Add people, make teams, share lists and assign tasks: see people, teams and sharing . # AI planning Describe what you need ( plan the launch next week ) and the AI drafts a nested plan for you to check before anything is added. Turn it on in Settings AI : local with Ollama, or Claude, ChatGPT or Gemini with your own key. See AI planning in the app . # Reminders Reminder pop-ups come from the Docket notifier, which runs in the background so they arrive even when the app is closed. They have Complete and Snooze 10 min buttons; clicking one opens the app. Installed with -Reminders ? It starts at sign-in already. Otherwise start it with docket notify --watch , or run the installer again with -Reminders . Settings Reminders can play a sound. Not seeing them? See troubleshooting . # Settings Section What's there General Confirm before deleting, where new tasks from smart lists go, theme (light, dark or system) AI Provider (Local, Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini or Off), model, API key, a test button Smart lists Show or hide Important, Planned, All, Completed; hide empty ones Reminders Sound on or off Connection Server address and API token, Test and save Keyboard shortcuts The full list ( shortcuts ) # Open in browser Open in browser opens Docket web ( http://localhost:3457 ) when it's running, or the server otherwise. # If the server isn't running When the task server on your PC has stopped, the app starts it again and carries on; you'll see Starting your task server… for a few seconds. If it can't, it offers Start server and retry . See if the server stops . # Repeats that only Docket understands Weekdays, yearly, and every-N-months or every-N-years repeats are handled by Docket itself (shown with a ↻ category). They move to the next date when you complete them in Docket, the CLI, Android or through the AI conn"},{"t":"Android app","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/android.html","h":[["Install and sign in","install-and-sign-in"],["Everyday use","everyday-use"],["People and sharing","people-and-sharing"],["Reminders","reminders"],["AI planning","ai-planning"],["Building it yourself","building-it-yourself"]],"x":"The Android app Docket for Android has the same features as the Windows app, in the same Fluent design, and works with the same tasks. # Install and sign in Install the APK from Releases (allow installing from your browser or file manager when Android asks), or from the Play Store once Docket is listed there. Open Docket and enter your server address : Windows install: the Tailscale address of your PC, like https://my-pc.tail1234.ts.net ( set it up ); Docker server: https://your-domain ; your own Vikunja: its address. Enter your Vikunja username and password. Docket makes a token for the phone; your password isn't stored. # Everyday use Home shows search, the smart lists with counts, and your lists and groups. Add a task with the add bar; quick-add words and commands work, including /ai . Swipe right on a task to add it to My Day; swipe left to delete it. Tap a task to open it: subtasks, checklist, My Day, reminder, due date, repeat (including custom), priority, categories, assignees, list, files (open and add), notes, delete. My Day suggestions , Planned grouped by date, sorting and a completed section work as on Windows. Views: List, Table, Timeline (the phone's Gantt) and Board for every list. Lists: rename, delete, icon, background, share. Theme: light, dark or system. # People and sharing Share lists with people and teams and assign tasks, as on Windows ( people, teams and sharing ). Creating brand-new local accounts for people is done from the Docket app on the PC that runs your server. # Reminders Reminders become Android notifications. They're scheduled each time you open Docket, so open the app now and then after adding reminders elsewhere (on the PC or through an AI). Allow notifications when Android asks; on some phones you also need to allow alarms and reminders and turn off battery optimisation for Docket. # AI planning Settings AI offers the same choices as Windows: Local AI on your PC (Ollama, over your network), Claude , ChatGPT , Gemini (with your API key, stored in Android's secure storage) or Off . See AI planning . # Building it yourself The app is .NET MAUI in src/Docket.Mobile . Build notes and the Play Store checklist: ANDROID.md ."},{"t":"Web app","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/web.html","h":[["What's different from the apps","whats-different-from-the-apps"],["Settings and API tokens","settings-and-api-tokens"]],"x":"Docket web Docket web is Vikunja's web app with the Docket look, for any computer or phone with a browser: a Mac, a Linux PC, an iPhone, or someone else's computer. Your setup Open Windows PC http://localhost:3457 (the app's Open in browser goes there) Windows PC, from your phone Your Tailscale address ( phone access ) Docker server https://your-domain Sign in with your Vikunja username and password. # What's different from the apps Docket web is Vikunja's own interface, reskinned, so it works a little differently from the Docket apps: It has Vikunja's views: list, Gantt, table and Kanban, plus Upcoming , teams, labels, sharing and settings. My Day shows as a label called My Day , and Important as a favourite (star). Docket's quick-add words and / commands are a Docket app feature; Vikunja has its own quick-add magic ( *label , +list , !priority , dates). Repeats that only Docket understands (weekdays, yearly, every N months or years; they carry a ↻ label) don't move forward when completed here. Complete those in a Docket app. Everything else, including tasks, subtasks, files and sharing, is the same data the apps see. # Settings and API tokens Vikunja's settings live here too: your profile, password, two-factor sign-in , and Settings API Tokens , where you can see and revoke the tokens Docket made for the app, the AI connector and each phone. Running and configuring Docket web: setup guide ."},{"t":"Quick add and commands","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/quick-add.html","h":[["Quick-add words","quick-add-words"],["Slash commands","slash-commands"],["The sparkle button","the-sparkle-button"]],"x":"Quick add and commands The add bar at the bottom of every list does three things: adds tasks, understands a few quick-add words, and runs commands that start with / . It works the same in the Windows and Android apps, and the same words work in the CLI and through the AI connector. # Quick-add words Type a task with a word or two for its date and priority. The words are taken out of the title. Type Sets today Due today tomorrow Due tomorrow a weekday: monday … sunday , or mon , tue , wed , thu , fri , sat , sun (also on friday ) Due on the next one (today's weekday means next week) in 3 days Due in that many days 20/11 Due on that day and month (day first). A date already past this year means next year !1 to !5 Priority: 1 low, 2 medium, 3 high, 4 urgent, 5 do now Quick-add due dates are set for 6 pm that day. Examples: You type You get Send invoice friday !3 Send invoice , due Friday, high priority Call the bank tomorrow Call the bank , due tomorrow Renew passport in 30 days !4 Renew passport , due in 30 days, urgent Dentist 20/11 Dentist , due 20 November Only one date is used, and the words must stand alone ( today's is left as it is). To set a time, reminder, repeat or star, open the task after adding it. # Slash commands Type / in the add bar to see the commands. Commands that act on tasks search first, show you what they found and ask you to confirm. Command Also What it does /s words /search Search tasks /x words /done Mark the tasks found done /m words /myday Add the tasks found to My Day /i words /star Star the tasks found (Important) /ai what you need Ask the AI to plan tasks ( AI planning ) /? /help , / Show the commands For example: /x invoice finds every open task with invoice and, after you confirm, marks them done. /m report puts all your report tasks in My Day. # The sparkle button The add bar has a sparkle button for AI. Turn it on and everything you type goes to the AI as a request, as if it started with /ai (choose an AI in Settings AI first). Turn it off to add tasks exactly as typed."},{"t":"Keyboard shortcuts","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/shortcuts.html","h":[],"x":"Keyboard shortcuts Docket for Windows can be used entirely from the keyboard. The same list is in the app under Settings Keyboard shortcuts . Keys What it does Ctrl + N Add a task (jump to the add bar) Ctrl + D Complete the selected task Ctrl + T Add the selected task to My Day, or remove it Ctrl + I Mark the selected task important, or not Delete Delete the selected task (with Undo) Enter Open the task's details Esc Close the details → / ← Expand or collapse subtasks Ctrl + F Search Ctrl + L New list Ctrl + R or F5 Refresh In the add bar, quick-add words set the date and priority, and / opens the commands. On this documentation site, press / to search."},{"t":"Views, boards and list styles","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/views-and-boards.html","h":[["Four views of every list","four-views-of-every-list"],["Boards","boards"],["List icons and backgrounds","list-icons-and-backgrounds"],["Sorting and filtering","sorting-and-filtering"],["Overview and Upcoming","overview-and-upcoming"]],"x":"Views, boards and list styles Every list can be shown four ways, and each list can have its own icon and background. # Four views of every list Switch with the view buttons at the top of a list. Each list remembers its view. View Good for List The everyday To Do view, with subtasks nested under their parents Table Many tasks at once, with columns for dates, priority and more Gantt Seeing work over time, using start and end dates (called Timeline on Android) Board Moving work through stages, Kanban style # Boards Every list has a board with To-Do , Doing and Done columns to start with. Add column at the end of the board makes a new one; add as many as you like. Each column's menu lets you rename it, pick a colour and an icon (a Fluent glyph or an emoji), or delete it. To-Do and Done always stay (you can rename them). Moving a card into Done completes the task. Add a card at the bottom of a column adds a task straight into it. Drag cards between columns. From the command line: docket board work shows a board and docket move 12 doing moves a task. An AI can use get_board and move_task . # List icons and backgrounds Open List options (the … at the top of a list): Icon… sets the list's icon: a Fluent glyph or any emoji. It shows in the sidebar and the list's title. Background… gives the list a backdrop: a colour , a gradient (with an angle), the accent colour, or an image from your PC. Colour sets the list's colour in the sidebar. # Sorting and filtering At the top of a list: Sort by my order, importance, due date, priority, alphabetically or creation date; show or hide completed tasks; search within the list; and Copy list to paste it as text anywhere. # Overview and Upcoming Overview is a dashboard of all your lists: how many tasks are open, due today and overdue, the last 14 days, tasks by priority, what's assigned to you and progress per list. docket overview and the AI tool overview give the same numbers. Upcoming shows what's due in the coming days, day by day. Open tasks shows everything unfinished across all lists."},{"t":"People, teams and sharing","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/people-and-sharing.html","h":[["Add people","add-people"],["Teams","teams"],["Share a list","share-a-list"],["Assign tasks","assign-tasks"],["What AI can and can't do","what-ai-can-and-cant-do"]],"x":"People, teams and sharing Docket lists can be shared, and tasks can be assigned to people. Everyone is an account on your own Docket server, so nothing goes through anyone else's cloud. # Add people Open People and teams in the sidebar. Someone who already has an account on your server: share a list with them (below) and they appear here. A new person : fill in Name , Email and Username under Add a person and choose Create account . Docket shows a password once ; send it to them with the server address so they can sign in to Docket (or Docket web) and change it. Creating accounts works when the server runs on the same PC as the app (the Windows install). For a Docker server, let people sign up while sign-ups are open, or create accounts with Vikunja's own tools. # Teams A team groups people so you can share a list with all of them at once. On the People page choose New team , name it and add members by username. # Share a list Open the list's List options Share list… . You can share with: Who How A person Type their username or email A team Choose the team Anyone with a link Public link , optionally with a password; no account needed Each person or team gets a permission: Permission They can Can view See the list and its tasks Can edit Add, change and complete tasks Can edit and share All of that, and share the list with others Shared lists show a sharing icon. Change someone's permission or stop sharing from the same dialog. # Assign tasks In a task's details, Assign to picks someone who can see the list. Assigning someone who can't see it yet shares the list with them first. Assigned tasks show their avatars on the row, and Overview lists what's assigned to you. From the command line: docket people # who you work with docket assign 12 pat # assign task 12 to pat docket unassign 12 pat docket share work # who can see the Work list docket share work pat edit # share it with pat (view, edit or admin) # What AI can and can't do An AI connected through the Docket connector can list people ( people ) and assign a task to someone who can already see its list ( assign ). It can't share lists, manage teams or create accounts: its token has no sharing rights, on purpose. Share from the app first, then ask the AI to assign."},{"t":"AI planning in the app","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/ai-planning.html","h":[["Use it","use-it"],["Choose an AI","choose-an-ai"],["Default models","default-models"],["Set up local AI with Ollama","set-up-local-ai-with-ollama"],["On Android","on-android"],["When it doesn't answer","when-it-doesnt-answer"]],"x":"AI planning in the app Describe what you need in plain words, and Docket's AI turns it into tasks, with subtasks, dates and priorities. You see the whole plan first; nothing is added until you confirm it. This is the AI inside the Docket apps. To let an assistant like Claude or ChatGPT work on your tasks from its own app, see AI assistants instead. # Use it In the add bar, type /ai and what you need, for example /ai plan the product launch next week: venue, invites, press kit . Or turn on the sparkle button in the add bar, and just type. Or from the command line: docket ai plan the launch next week shows the plan; run it again with --yes to add it. The AI keeps your own words and names, only sets dates you gave or clearly implied, breaks big jobs into subtasks, and can put things in My Day or mark them important. You can paste a messy list or meeting notes too. # Choose an AI Open Settings AI and pick a provider: Provider What you need Where your text goes Local Ollama on your PC and a downloaded model Nowhere: it stays on your PC Claude An API key from console.anthropic.com Anthropic ChatGPT An API key from platform.openai.com OpenAI Gemini An API key from aistudio.google.com Google Off Nothing The add bar adds tasks exactly as typed Then choose Test: plan Buy milk tomorrow to check it works. API keys are stored encrypted for your Windows account (on Android, in the system's secure storage) and only ever sent to that provider. API use is billed by the provider, separately from any Claude or ChatGPT subscription; a planning request is small. # Default models Provider Default model Change it Local (Ollama) qwen2.5:7b , or another chat model you have installed Pick from the Model list Claude claude-haiku-5-5 Type another model name in Model ChatGPT gpt-4.1-mini Same Gemini gemini-2.5-flash Same # Set up local AI with Ollama Local AI is free and private, but needs a reasonably capable PC: a 7–8B model wants about 8 GB of free memory, and runs much faster with a recent graphics card. In Settings AI , choose Local . Docket checks for Ollama. If it says Ollama isn't installed , choose Install Ollama . Docket installs it for your account with the Windows Package Manager (winget); a window shows the progress. You can also install it yourself from ollama.com . If it says Ollama is installed but not running , choose Start Ollama (it then runs from the system tray). When it says Local AI is running but no model is installed, type a model name (the box suggests qwen2.5:7b ) and choose Download model . It's several GB and stays on your PC. Choose the model in the Model list, then Test . The first answer after starting can take up to a minute while the model loads. Good small models for planning: qwen2.5:7b , qwen3:8b , llama3.1:8b . Very small models (under about 4B) make messier plans. From a terminal, the same is winget install Ollama.Ollama and ollama pull qwen2.5:7b . # On Android The phone uses the same choices. Local AI on your PC reaches Ollama over your network, by default at your Docket server's address on port 11434 (you can change it in Settings AI). For that to work: Make Ollama listen on the network, not only on the PC: set the Windows environment variable OLLAMA_HOST to 0.0.0.0 (Settings System About Advanced system settings Environment Variables), then quit and restart Ollama from the tray. If Windows Firewall asks, allow Ollama on private networks. With Tailscale, the phone reaches the PC at its Tailscale name, so the default address works. Warning OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 lets other devices on your network use Ollama. That's fine at home or on Tailscale; don't do it on public Wi-Fi. # When it doesn't answer See troubleshooting: AI planning doesn't respond ."},{"t":"Command line (CLI)","s":"Use Docket","u":"using/cli.html","h":[["Everyday commands","everyday-commands"],["See tasks","see-tasks"],["Add and change tasks","add-and-change-tasks"],["My Day, stars, steps, repeats and reminders","my-day-stars-steps-repeats-and-reminders"],["Subtasks","subtasks"],["People and sharing","people-and-sharing"],["Overview and AI","overview-and-ai"],["Lists and boards","lists-and-boards"],["Reminders and setup","reminders-and-setup"],["Quick-add words","quick-add-words"],["JSON for scripts","json-for-scripts"],["Full help","full-help"]],"x":"The command line The docket command shows and changes your tasks from a terminal on Windows. It's installed by the Windows installer and added to your PATH; open a new terminal after installing. docket # today and overdue docket add Send invoice friday !3 -p Work docket done 12 Task ids are the numbers shown as #12 . Lists can be given by name or id. # Everyday commands # See tasks docket # due today and overdue (same as: docket today) docket myday # My Day docket suggest # suggestions for My Day docket important # starred docket planned # with a due date, grouped Earlier, Today, Tomorrow, This week, Later docket all # every open task docket completed # finished tasks docket upcoming # next 7 days docket overdue docket open # everything unfinished docket done # recently finished docket search invoice # search titles and notes (--all includes finished tasks) docket show 12 # one task in full Options for listings: -p list narrows to one list, -n number limits how many, --json prints JSON. # Add and change tasks docket add Send invoice friday !3 -p work -l admin # quick-add words, list, category docket add Plan Q1 -d 2026-12-01 -P 2 --desc Draft first docket done 12 14 # complete (repeating tasks move to their next date) docket undo 14 # reopen docket edit 12 -t New title -d none -P 2 # title, due (none clears), priority 0-5 docket notes 12 Use the new template # replace notes (steps are kept) docket comment 12 Called, waiting for PO docket move 12 doing # board column docket delete 12 --yes # without --yes it asks first # My Day, stars, steps, repeats and reminders docket myday add 12 15 docket myday rm 12 docket star 12 docket unstar 12 docket step 12 add Price it docket step 12 done 1 docket step 12 undo 1 docket step 12 rm 1 docket repeat 12 weekdays # daily, weekdays, weekly, monthly, yearly, every 3 weeks , none docket remind 12 tomorrow 9:00 # in 2 hours , fri 5pm , 2026-11-20T09:00, none docket cat 12 Client Urgent # set categories (none clears) docket label 12 Admin # add one category # Subtasks docket sub 12 Book the venue friday # add a subtask under #12 docket parent 15 12 # also put #15 under #12 (a task can have several parents) docket parent 15 rm 12 # take it out again docket tree work # a list with subtasks nested docket convert 12 # turn old checklist steps into subtasks # People and sharing docket people docket assign 12 pat docket unassign 12 pat docket share work # who can see it docket share work pat edit # share with a username or email: view, edit or admin # Overview and AI docket overview # dashboard numbers docket ai plan the launch next week # show a plan from the AI chosen in Settings AI docket ai plan the launch next week --yes # show it and add it # Lists and boards docket projects # all lists with counts docket board work # a list's board docket list add Clients --group Studio docket list rename Studio Studio work docket list color Studio #4F8BFF # Reminders and setup docket notify --watch # show reminder notifications all day (Ctrl+C stops) docket notify --once # show what's due now, then exit docket status # which server, its Vikunja version and how many lists docket login --server http://localhost:3456 # connect with an API token docket notify --watch shows a Windows notification when a reminder falls due, with Complete and Snooze 10 min buttons; clicking it opens the app. The installer's -Reminders option starts it at sign-in. If the task server on your PC stops, it starts it again and keeps watching. # Quick-add words today , tomorrow , weekdays ( fri ), in 3 days , 20/11 (day/month) set the due date, and !1 to !5 the priority. See quick add . # JSON for scripts Add --json to any listing: docket today --json | ConvertFrom-Json | Where-Object priority -ge 3 docket planned --json # [{ group: Today , task: {...} }, ...] docket show 42 --json The task format is in the developer API . Exit codes: 0 success, 1 error, 2 wrong usage. # Full help This is the output of docket --help , kept in sync with the program: !-- sync:cli -- docket: your Vikunja tasks from the terminal Lists docket [today] due today and overdue docket myday My Day (resets each day) docket suggest suggestions for My Day docket important | planned | all | completed docket upcoming | overdue | open | done docket search words [--all] search titles and notes (--all includes done) options: -p project -n limit --json Tasks docket add text [-p list] [-d due] [-P 0-5] [--desc text] [-l label]... quick-add words work in text : Call Pat friday !3 docket show id docket done id [ id ...] docket undo id (repeating tasks move to their next date) docket myday add|rm id ... docket star|unstar id ... docket step id add text docket step id done|undo|rm n docket repeat id daily|weekdays|weekly|monthly|yearly| every 3 weeks |none docket remind id when |none when: tomorrow 9:00 , in 2 hours , fri 5pm , 2026-11-20T09:00 docket cat id category ... set categories (none clears) docket notes id text replace notes (steps are kept) docket edit id [-t title] [-d due|none] [-P 0-5] [--desc text] [-p list] docket move id column e.g. docket move 12 doing docket comment id text docket label id label docket delete id [--yes] Subtasks (real tasks, any depth, a task can have several parents) docket sub parent id text add a subtask docket tree [list] show a list nested docket parent id parent id also put it under another task docket parent id rm parent id docket convert id old steps - subtasks People and sharing docket people docket assign|unassign id username docket share list who can see it docket share list username|email [view|edit|admin] Overview and AI docket overview dashboard numbers docket ai what you need [--yes] plan tasks with the AI set in the Docket app's Settings Lists and boards docket projects docket board list docket list add name [--group list ] docket list rename list new name docket list color list #hex Reminders docket notify --once show reminders that are due now docket notify --watch keep showing them (Ctr"},{"t":"How AI works with Docket","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/index.html","h":[["Pick your setup","pick-your-setup"],["The two ways to run the connector","the-two-ways-to-run-the-connector"],["Settings (environment variables)","settings-environment-variables"],["Tools","tools"],["Prompts that work well","prompts-that-work-well"]],"x":"Docket with AI assistants Docket ships one connector, docket-mcp , that lets AI assistants read and change your tasks. It speaks the Model Context Protocol (MCP), so any MCP-capable assistant can use it, and it also offers the same tools as a plain REST API with an OpenAPI description for assistants that use actions instead. Nothing the connector does can delete. None of its 26 tools delete, and the Vikunja token the setup scripts give it has no delete, sharing or team rights either. Deleting and sharing stay in the Docket apps, where you can undo them. # Pick your setup You use How it connects Guide Claude Desktop, Claude Code Local (stdio), on the same computer as your Docket settings Claude claude.ai on the web or phone Remote MCP over HTTPS, OAuth sign-in Claude ChatGPT Remote MCP connector (developer mode) or a custom GPT with actions ChatGPT Gemini CLI, Copilot, Cursor, VS Code, other MCP clients Local (stdio) or remote MCP Other MCP clients LM Studio, Ollama, Jan, Open WebUI (local models) Local (stdio), fully offline Local models Your own scripts or agents REST, OpenAPI, MCP or the docket CLI Developer API Using Claude? Add the Docket skill too: it teaches Claude how to use these tools well. Note This page is about assistants working on your tasks from their apps. To plan tasks with AI from inside the Docket apps (the /ai command), see AI planning in the app . # The two ways to run the connector Local (stdio). The assistant starts docket-mcp itself and talks to it over standard input/output. Nothing listens on the network. Use this for desktop apps on the computer where Docket is set up. Windows: the installer (or deploy\\windows\\setup-tokens.ps1 ) puts it at %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Programs\\Docket\\mcp\\docket-mcp.exe and connects it for you. It reads your Docket settings. macOS and Linux: download docket-mcp for your platform from the releases , then give it your Vikunja address and a token with environment variables (below). Remote (HTTPS). docket-mcp --http serves MCP at /mcp , REST at /api/tools/{name} , an OpenAPI description at /openapi.json , and an OAuth sign-in at /oauth/* . Web and phone assistants (claude.ai, ChatGPT) need this, because they connect from the internet. The Docker server runs it behind HTTPS for you. Remote clients prove who they are in one of two ways, both tied to your connector key ( DOCKET_MCP_KEY ): Bearer key: send Authorization: Bearer connector key . For clients that let you set headers. OAuth: for clients that only do OAuth (claude.ai, ChatGPT). They register themselves, open Docket's sign-in page, you type the connector key once, and they get tokens that refresh on their own. The sign-in page shows which address access will go to; only approve a connection you started yourself. Changing the key revokes every OAuth sign-in. # Settings (environment variables) Variable Used by Meaning DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL both Your Vikunja address, e.g. http://localhost:3456 or https://tasks.example.com DOCKET_VIKUNJA_TOKEN both A Vikunja API token. Prefer one without delete rights ( docket-mcp setup makes one) DOCKET_VIKUNJA_TOKEN_FILE both Read the token from this file instead (default: DOCKET_DATA_DIR/vikunja-token ) DOCKET_DEFAULT_LIST both List id new tasks go to when none is named DOCKET_DATA_DIR both Where the connector keeps state.json , oauth.json and the token file (default ./data ) DOCKET_MCP_KEY remote The connector key, 24+ characters ( openssl rand -hex 32 ) DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL remote The public HTTPS address, e.g. https://tasks.example.com , used in OAuth metadata DOCKET_MCP_URLS remote Listen address (default http://127.0.0.1:8080 ; the Docker image uses 0.0.0.0:8080 ) DOCKET_OAUTH_REDIRECT_HOSTS remote Hosts OAuth may send you back to. Default: claude.ai, claude.com, chatgpt.com, chat.openai.com, platform.openai.com (localhost is always allowed). Comma-separated; * allows any https host TZ both Time zone for today , due dates and reminders On Windows, when the Vikunja variables aren't set, the connector uses the Docket settings file instead ( %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\settings.json , protected with Windows DPAPI). To make a token without the Docket app: docket-mcp setup asks for your Vikunja username and password once, creates a token called Docket AI connector with no delete rights, and saves it to the token file. Revoke it any time in Vikunja under Settings API Tokens . # Tools Tool What it does list_tasks Tasks by view: today, upcoming, overdue, open, done, my_day, important, planned, all, completed; optional list and search get_task One task in full: steps, notes, reminders, repeat, categories, files, board column, comments add_task Add a task; the title can carry quick-add words ( tomorrow , fri , in 3 days , 20/11 , !1 – !5 ) update_task Change title, due date, priority or list complete_task Complete or reopen tasks; repeating tasks move to their next date move_task Move to a Kanban column (Done completes it) add_comment Comment on a task add_label / set_categories Add one category, or replace them all list_projects / get_board Lists with counts and columns; one list's board create_project / create_list New list, optionally inside a group my_day / set_my_day My Day (with suggestions); add or remove tasks set_important Star or unstar steps Add, complete, uncomplete or remove checklist steps set_repeat daily, weekdays, weekly, monthly, yearly, every N days/weeks/months/years , none set_reminder Replace reminders ( tomorrow 9:00 , in 2 hours , fri 5pm , ISO times) set_notes Replace the notes, keeping steps add_subtask / link_subtask Add a subtask, or put an existing task under another (several parents allowed) task_tree A list nested, with subtasks under their parents assign / people Assign a task to someone who can see its list; list the people you work with overview Dashboard numbers: open, due today, overdue, next 7 days, done this week, by priority and list Every result is readable text with task ids written as #123 , which keeps small local models on track t"},{"t":"Claude","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/claude.html","h":[["Claude Desktop","claude-desktop"],["Claude Code","claude-code"],["claude.ai (web, desktop and phone)","claudeai-web-desktop-and-phone"],["Troubleshooting","troubleshooting"]],"x":"Docket with Claude Three ways in, depending on where you use Claude. You can use more than one; they all work on the same tasks. Once connected, add the Docket skill so Claude knows how to work with your tasks. Where Setup Needs Claude Desktop Local connector Docket set up on that computer Claude Code Local or remote connector Docket on that computer, or a Docket server claude.ai (web, desktop, phone) Remote connector A Docket server reachable from the internet # Claude Desktop Windows, with the installer: nothing to do. install.ps1 (through setup-tokens.ps1 ) adds a docket entry to Claude Desktop's config. Quit Claude Desktop fully (tray icon Quit) and reopen it; you'll see Docket's tools under the tools icon in the message box. By hand (any OS), edit claude_desktop_config.json (Claude Desktop Settings Developer Edit Config ): { mcpServers : { docket : { command : C:\\\\Users\\\\you\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Programs\\\\Docket\\\\mcp\\\\docket-mcp.exe } } } On macOS or Linux, point command at the downloaded docket-mcp and pass your server and token: { mcpServers : { docket : { command : /usr/local/bin/docket-mcp , env : { DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL : https://tasks.example.com , DOCKET_VIKUNJA_TOKEN_FILE : /Users/you/.config/docket/vikunja-token } } } } Make the token file once with DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL=https://tasks.example.com DOCKET_DATA_DIR=~/.config/docket docket-mcp setup . Save the config without a byte-order mark (plain UTF-8); Claude Desktop rejects files that start with one. If you run a Docket server, Claude Desktop can also use the remote connector : connectors you add on claude.ai show up in the desktop app too. # Claude Code Local (same computer as Docket): claude mcp add --scope user docket -- C:\\Users\\you\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Docket\\mcp\\docket-mcp.exe --scope user makes it available in every project. On macOS/Linux add -e DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL=... -e DOCKET_VIKUNJA_TOKEN_FILE=... before -- . Remote (a Docket server), with the connector key as a header: claude mcp add --scope user --transport http docket https://tasks.example.com/mcp \\ --header Authorization: Bearer YOUR_CONNECTOR_KEY Or leave the header off and run /mcp in Claude Code to sign in with OAuth instead. Check it with claude mcp get docket , or /mcp inside a session. # claude.ai (web, desktop and phone) This uses a custom connector , which works on every claude.ai plan (Free allows one custom connector). Claude connects to your server from Anthropic's cloud, so the server needs a public HTTPS address. The Docker server gives you one; Tailscale Funnel or a Cloudflare Tunnel work if you don't want to open ports. In Claude, go to Customize Connectors and choose Add custom connector . (On Team and Enterprise plans an Owner adds it under Organization settings Connectors , then each member connects.) Name: Docket . URL: https://tasks.example.com/mcp . Authentication: Sign in now (or Sign in when needed ). OAuth client: Register automatically . Leave client ID and secret empty. Click Add , then Connect . Docket's sign-in page opens: type your connector key ( DOCKET_MCP_KEY from the server's .env ) and choose Allow access . In a chat, open + Connectors and make sure Docket is on. The connection then works in the Claude apps on your phone and desktop as well. Claude asks before using a tool that changes something; you can allow Docket's tools permanently, or block single tools, under Customize Connectors Docket . If your organization has request-header authentication, you can choose No sign-in and add a request header authorization with the value Bearer YOUR_CONNECTOR_KEY instead of OAuth. To disconnect: remove the connector in Claude. To cut off every OAuth client at once, change DOCKET_MCP_KEY and restart the connector; every sign-in made with the old key stops working. # Troubleshooting Symptom Fix Tools don't appear in Claude Desktop Quit it from the tray icon, not just the window. Check the path in the config, and that the file has no BOM. Docket isn't set up yet The connector can't find a token: sign in with the Docket app or installer (Windows), or set DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL and a token. claude.ai: couldn't connect Open https://your-domain/connector/health in a browser; it should say status : ok . The server must be reachable from the internet, not just your network. Sign-in page says the link isn't valid Remove the connector in Claude and add it again (its registration was lost, e.g. oauth.json was deleted). Too many wrong keys Ten wrong keys from one address in 15 minutes locks sign-in for 15 minutes. isn't an allowed return address The client sends you back somewhere Docket doesn't know; add its host to DOCKET_OAUTH_REDIRECT_HOSTS . Times are off by hours Set TZ on the server (the Docker .env has it)."},{"t":"Claude skill","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/claude-skill.html","h":[["What's in it","whats-in-it"],["Install in claude.ai and the Claude apps","install-in-claudeai-and-the-claude-apps"],["Install in Claude Code","install-in-claude-code"],["Try it","try-it"],["Using it with other assistants","using-it-with-other-assistants"],["Updating","updating"]],"x":"The Docket skill for Claude The Docket skill teaches Claude how to work well with your tasks: which Docket tool to use for what's on today or plan my day , how to break work into nested subtasks, when to ask before changing many tasks, and what to tell you when you ask for something the connector can't do (like deleting). It's free and open source, like Docket. Download docket-skill.zip The skill is instructions, not a connection. It pairs with the Docket connector : connect Docket first ( Claude ), then add the skill. Without a connector, Claude can still use the skill with the docket command line in Claude Code on Windows. # What's in it File What docket/SKILL.md When to use the skill, the ground rules, common requests mapped to tools, planning steps, CLI fallback, example prompts docket/reference/tools.md All 26 connector tools and their arguments, generated from Docket's source Read it on GitHub: skills/docket/SKILL.md . The skill's description, which tells Claude when to use it: Manage the user's tasks and to-dos in Docket, a self-hosted Microsoft To Do style app on Vikunja, through the Docket connector's tools (list_tasks, my_day, add_task...) or the docket CLI. Use when the user asks what's on today, wants to plan their day, week or a project, or to add, find, change, complete or organise tasks, subtasks, My Day, important items, due dates, reminders, repeats, lists, boards, assignments or the overview, or mentions Docket or their to-do list. # Install in claude.ai and the Claude apps Skills you upload on claude.ai also work in the Claude desktop and mobile apps. Make sure code execution is on: Settings Capabilities Code execution and file creation (on Team and Enterprise plans an owner turns on skills for the organization). Download docket-skill.zip . Don't unzip it. In Claude, open the Skills settings and choose to upload a skill , then pick the zip. Turn the Docket skill on. Claude's menus change from time to time; Anthropic's help article Use skills in Claude has the current steps and plan requirements. # Install in Claude Code Claude Code reads skills from folders. Unzip docket-skill.zip and copy the docket folder to either: For Folder Every project ~/.claude/skills/docket/ (Windows: `%USERPROFILE%.claude\\skills\\docket`) One project (shared with the repo) .claude/skills/docket/ in that project So that you end up with ~/.claude/skills/docket/SKILL.md . From a clone of the Docket repository you can copy it directly: mkdir -p ~/.claude/skills cp -r skills/docket ~/.claude/skills/ New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $env:USERPROFILE\\.claude\\skills | Out-Null Copy-Item -Recurse skills\\docket $env:USERPROFILE\\.claude\\skills\\ Start a new Claude Code session; the skill is used automatically when you talk about your tasks. Connect Docket too, locally or remotely: Claude Code setup . # Try it What's on my plate today? Put the three most urgent in My Day. Plan the product launch next week as a project with subtasks, then show me the tree. Break #42 into subtasks: research, draft, review, send. Add 'Send the Q3 invoice' to Work, due Friday, high priority, and remind me Thursday at 4pm. How did this week go? # Using it with other assistants SKILL.md is plain Markdown. For ChatGPT, a custom GPT or a local model, paste its sections 2 to 5 into the instructions or system prompt; they work the same with any assistant that has the Docket tools. # Updating When a new Docket release adds tools, download the new zip and upload it again (or copy the folder again). The tool reference inside is regenerated with every release."},{"t":"ChatGPT","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/chatgpt.html","h":[["Option 1: MCP connector (developer mode)","option-1-mcp-connector-developer-mode"],["Option 2: Custom GPT with actions","option-2-custom-gpt-with-actions"],["Other online assistants","other-online-assistants"],["Troubleshooting","troubleshooting"]],"x":"Docket with ChatGPT ChatGPT connects from OpenAI's cloud, so you need a Docket server with a public HTTPS address. There are two ways to hook it up; pick by what your plan offers. MCP connector (developer mode) Custom GPT with actions Uses https://your-domain/mcp https://your-domain/openapi.json Sign-in OAuth (you type the connector key once) Bearer key stored in the GPT Works in Any chat where you turn the connector on Only inside that GPT Availability Depends on your plan; OpenAI has offered full (write) MCP access in developer mode on Business, Enterprise and Edu, and more limited access on other plans. Check OpenAI's help for your plan. Plans that can create GPTs OpenAI changes these menus often; if a name below doesn't match, search OpenAI's help for developer mode or GPT actions . # Option 1: MCP connector (developer mode) In ChatGPT, open Settings Apps Connectors Advanced settings and turn on Developer mode . (On workspace plans an admin may need to allow it.) Back in Apps Connectors , choose Create (or Add custom connector ). Name: Docket . MCP server URL: https://tasks.example.com/mcp . Authentication: OAuth . ChatGPT registers itself with Docket automatically (dynamic client registration); leave client ID/secret empty. Docket's sign-in page opens. Type your connector key ( DOCKET_MCP_KEY ) and choose Allow access . In a chat, open the + menu, pick Developer mode (or More Docket ) and ask about your tasks. ChatGPT asks you to confirm before it uses a tool that changes something. Docket marks its read tools as read-only, so listing and reading need no confirmation. ChatGPT can't send custom headers to MCP servers, so the bearer-key method doesn't apply here; OAuth is required. # Option 2: Custom GPT with actions Go to Explore GPTs Create , then the Configure tab. Name it (e.g. My tasks ). Instructions, for example: You manage my to-do list through the Docket actions. Task ids appear as #123; use them in follow-up calls. Use list_tasks with view=today to see what's due, my_day for My Day. Never invent ids; look tasks up first. Under Actions , choose Create new action , then Import from URL and enter https://tasks.example.com/openapi.json . ChatGPT reads all 26 operations ( the tools ). Authentication : API Key , Auth Type Bearer , and paste your connector key. Privacy policy : required only if you publish the GPT. Keep it Only me so nobody else can use your key. Save, then test in the preview pane: What's due today? Operations that change data are marked as consequential, so ChatGPT asks before running them. # Other online assistants The OpenAPI description at /openapi.json works with any assistant or automation tool that imports OpenAPI 3.1 (for example Gemini Gems and Microsoft Copilot Studio, Zapier, n8n or Make), with Bearer authentication. For assistants that speak MCP, see other-clients.md . # Troubleshooting Symptom Fix Error fetching OAuth configuration https://your-domain/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server must load from the internet. If you changed the domain, set DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL to the new address. Action import fails Open https://your-domain/openapi.json in a browser; check the servers URL is your public HTTPS address (set DOCKET_PUBLIC_URL ). 401 from an action The Auth Type must be Bearer and the key must match DOCKET_MCP_KEY exactly. New tools don't show up ChatGPT keeps a connector's tool list from when it was created; delete and re-add the connector after upgrading Docket."},{"t":"Local models","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/local-ai.html","h":[["LM Studio","lm-studio"],["Ollama","ollama"],["Run the connector over HTTP on your network","run-the-connector-over-http-on-your-network"],["Tips for small models","tips-for-small-models"]],"x":"Docket with local AI (LM Studio, Ollama, Jan, Open WebUI) You can run your task assistant entirely on your own computer: a local model, a local MCP client, the local Docket connector and your own Vikunja. Nothing leaves your machine or network. What you need Docket set up (the Windows installer, or a Docket server on your network). A model that's good at tool calling. Recent instruction-tuned models with tool support work best, for example Qwen 3 (8B or larger), Llama 3.1/3.3 (8B or larger), Mistral Small or gpt-oss. Very small models (under ~4B) often call tools with wrong arguments. An app that connects models to MCP servers. Ollama itself doesn't load MCP servers; pair it with one of the clients below. # LM Studio LM Studio is both the model runner and an MCP client. Load a model with tool use (the model list shows a hammer icon for tool-capable models). In the right sidebar open Program Install Edit mcp.json and add Docket: { mcpServers : { docket : { command : C:\\\\Users\\\\you\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Programs\\\\Docket\\\\mcp\\\\docket-mcp.exe } } } On macOS/Linux add env : { DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL : http://localhost:3456 , DOCKET_VIKUNJA_TOKEN_FILE : /home/you/.config/docket/vikunja-token } . For a Docket server instead: docket : { url : https://tasks.example.com/mcp , headers : { Authorization : Bearer YOUR_CONNECTOR_KEY } } . Save. In a chat, LM Studio asks before each tool call; approve it, or allow Docket's read-only tools permanently. # Ollama Use Ollama for the model and one of these as the MCP client: Open WebUI (browser UI for Ollama): in Admin Settings External Tools , add a server of type MCP (Streamable HTTP) with URL http://your-docket-server:8080/mcp (or https://tasks.example.com/mcp ) and a Bearer key. Then turn the tool on in a chat. Open WebUI calls HTTP servers, so run the connector with --http ( below ). Jan, Msty, Cherry Studio, 5ire and other desktop chat apps with MCP support: add a local (stdio) server with the docket-mcp command, like the LM Studio example, and pick an Ollama model. Continue (VS Code / JetBrains) : add Docket under mcpServers in Continue's config and choose an Ollama model. Terminal : ollmcp connects Ollama models to MCP servers; give it the docket-mcp command as a server (see its README for the current flags). # Run the connector over HTTP on your network Some local clients only speak HTTP. Run the connector on the machine with Docket: # Windows, with your Docket settings $env:DOCKET_MCP_KEY = a-long-random-key-of-24-or-more-characters $env:DOCKET_MCP_URLS = http://127.0.0.1:8080 # use 0.0.0.0:8080 to allow other devices on your network $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\mcp\\docket-mcp.exe --http Then use http://127.0.0.1:8080/mcp with Authorization: Bearer that key . Plain HTTP is fine on one computer; across a network, put it behind HTTPS (Tailscale, Caddy) or use the Docker server . # Tips for small models Name the tool in your request: Use list_tasks with view today. Small models follow explicit tool names well. Keep the conversation short; start a new chat for a new job so the tool list and results fit the context window. Give the model a system prompt such as: You manage the user's to-do list with the Docket tools. Task ids look like #123. Look tasks up before changing them, never guess ids, and confirm what you changed in one short sentence. Raise the context length to at least 8k tokens; Docket's 26 tool descriptions take about 5k."},{"t":"Other MCP clients","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/other-clients.html","h":[["Gemini CLI","gemini-cli"],["VS Code (GitHub Copilot agent mode)","vs-code-github-copilot-agent-mode"],["Cursor, Windsurf and other mcp.json clients","cursor-windsurf-and-other-mcpjson-clients"],["Clients that only do OAuth","clients-that-only-do-oauth"],["Checking a connection","checking-a-connection"]],"x":"Docket with other MCP clients Any app that supports MCP can use Docket. Use the local form when the app runs on the computer where Docket is set up, and the remote form for a Docket server . Replace the paths, address and key with yours. Config formats change between versions; if one below is rejected, check the app's own MCP docs. Local command, by platform: Windows (installer): C:\\Users\\you\\AppData\\Local\\Programs\\Docket\\mcp\\docket-mcp.exe (reads your Docket settings) macOS / Linux: the docket-mcp you downloaded, with DOCKET_VIKUNJA_URL and DOCKET_VIKUNJA_TOKEN_FILE set ( how ) # Gemini CLI ~/.gemini/settings.json : { mcpServers : { docket : { command : C:\\\\Users\\\\you\\\\AppData\\\\Local\\\\Programs\\\\Docket\\\\mcp\\\\docket-mcp.exe } } } Remote: { mcpServers : { docket : { httpUrl : https://tasks.example.com/mcp , headers : { Authorization : Bearer YOUR_CONNECTOR_KEY } } } } Run /mcp in Gemini CLI to see Docket's tools. # VS Code (GitHub Copilot agent mode) .vscode/mcp.json in a workspace, or MCP: Open User Configuration for all workspaces: { servers : { docket : { type : http , url : https://tasks.example.com/mcp , headers : { Authorization : Bearer ${input:docketKey} } } }, inputs : [ { id : docketKey , type : promptString , description : Docket connector key , password : true } ] } Local: docket : { type : stdio , command : C:\\...\\docket-mcp.exe } . # Cursor, Windsurf and other mcp.json clients ~/.cursor/mcp.json (Cursor), and the same shape in most clients that copied it: { mcpServers : { docket : { url : https://tasks.example.com/mcp , headers : { Authorization : Bearer YOUR_CONNECTOR_KEY } } } } Local: docket : { command : C:\\...\\docket-mcp.exe , env : {} } . # Clients that only do OAuth Point them at https://tasks.example.com/mcp . Docket advertises its OAuth server through the standard MCP discovery ( /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource ), supports dynamic client registration and PKCE, and its sign-in page asks for your connector key once. Return addresses must be http(s)://localhost (desktop apps) or https on a host in DOCKET_OAUTH_REDIRECT_HOSTS (by default the Claude and ChatGPT sites); add your client's host there if it's refused. # Checking a connection # Is the connector up, and can it reach Vikunja? curl https://tasks.example.com/connector/health # Does the key work? (should list 26 tools) curl -s https://tasks.example.com/mcp -H Authorization: Bearer YOUR_CONNECTOR_KEY \\ -H Content-Type: application/json -H Accept: application/json, text/event-stream \\ -d '{ jsonrpc : 2.0 , id :1, method : tools/list }' The MCP Inspector ( npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector ) is handy for trying tools by hand, locally or remotely."},{"t":"Tool reference","s":"AI assistants","u":"ai/tools.html","h":[["Tasks, lists and boards","tasks-lists-and-boards"],["list_tasks","list_tasks"],["get_task","get_task"],["add_task","add_task"],["update_task","update_task"],["complete_task","complete_task"],["move_task","move_task"],["add_comment","add_comment"],["add_label","add_label"],["list_projects","list_projects"],["get_board","get_board"],["create_project","create_project"],["My Day, stars, steps, repeats and reminders","my-day-stars-steps-repeats-and-reminders"],["my_day","my_day"],["set_my_day","set_my_day"],["set_important","set_important"],["steps","steps"],["set_repeat","set_repeat"],["set_reminder","set_reminder"],["set_categories","set_categories"],["set_notes","set_notes"],["create_list","create_list"],["Subtasks, people and the overview","subtasks-people-and-the-overview"],["add_subtask","add_subtask"],["link_subtask","link_subtask"],["task_tree","task_tree"],["assign","assign"],["people","people"],["overview","overview"]],"x":"Tool reference These are the tools the Docket connector ( docket-mcp ) gives an AI assistant. The same tools are available over MCP, and as REST at POST /api/tools/{name} ( developer API ). A few things hold for all of them: Nothing deletes or shares. Deleting tasks or lists, sharing lists and managing teams are left to the Docket apps, and the connector's token couldn't do them anyway. Results are readable text , with task ids written as #123 . Use those ids in follow-up calls. Dates accept 2026-11-20 , 2026-11-20T15:00 , today , tomorrow , a weekday ( fri ), in 3 days , 20/11 (day/month) or none to clear. Lists (Vikunja calls them projects) can be named or given by id. Read-only tools are marked as such to MCP clients ( readOnlyHint ), so Claude and ChatGPT can run them without asking. Changing tools ask first, unless you allow them. !-- sync:tools -- Docket's connector has 26 tools : 8 only read, and 18 change tasks. None delete or share. Tool Changes data In short list_tasks no List the user's tasks. get_task no Full details of one task: important, My Day, due date, repeat, reminders, priority, categories, steps (numbered), notes, file attachments, its Kanban column and the board's columns, and comments. add_task yes Add a task. update_task yes Change a task's title, due date, priority or list. complete_task yes Mark one or more tasks done, or reopen them with done=false. move_task yes Move a task to a column of its list's Kanban board, by column name (e.g. add_comment yes Add a comment to a task, for progress notes or context. add_label yes Attach one category (label) to a task, keeping its others. list_projects no All lists (projects, with groups shown as Parent / Child) with their open-task counts and Kanban columns. get_board no A list's Kanban board: each column with its tasks. create_project yes Create a list (a project; it gets a list view and a Kanban board with To-Do, Doing and Done). my_day no The user's My Day list (resets each day), with its completed tasks. set_my_day yes Add tasks to My Day (in_my_day=true, default) or remove them (false). set_important yes Mark tasks important (starred) or not. steps yes Work with a task's steps (a checklist inside the task). set_repeat yes Make a task repeat, or stop it repeating. set_reminder yes Replace a task's reminders. set_categories yes Replace a task's categories (labels) with these names; missing ones are created. set_notes yes Replace a task's notes (plain text). create_list yes Create a list, optionally inside a group (an existing list used as its parent). add_subtask yes Add a subtask under a task. link_subtask yes Make an existing task a subtask of another one too. task_tree no A list's open tasks as a tree, with subtasks indented under their parents (a task with two parents appears under both). assign yes Assign a task to a person who can already see its list (see the people tool). people no Everyone the user works with on their lists (usernames and names). overview no Dashboard numbers: open, due today, overdue, next 7 days, done this week, by priority and per list. # Tasks, lists and boards # list_tasks List the user's tasks. Views: today (due today or overdue), upcoming (next 7 days), overdue, open (everything unfinished), done (recently finished), my_day (the user's My Day list), important (starred), planned (has a due date, grouped Earlier/Today/Tomorrow/This week/Later), all (every open task), completed. Optionally narrow to one list (project) or search words. Lines show ★ for important, My Day, steps progress, ↻ repeat and ⏰ next reminder. Read-only. view (text): today | upcoming | overdue | open | done | my_day | important | planned | all | completed. Default today. project (text): List (project) name or id to narrow to. Optional. search (text): Words to search in titles and notes. Optional. limit (integer): Maximum tasks to return, 1-200. Default 50. # get_task Full details of one task: important, My Day, due date, repeat, reminders, priority, categories, steps (numbered), notes, file attachments, its Kanban column and the board's columns, and comments. Read-only. id (integer, required): Task id, e.g. 12 # add_task Add a task. The title may include quick-add words, which are taken out of the title: today, tomorrow, a weekday, in 3 days , 20/11 for the due date and !1 to !5 for priority ( Send invoice friday !3 ). Without a list it goes to the user's default list (usually Inbox). Use set_my_day, set_important, steps, set_repeat and set_reminder afterwards for the rest. Changes data. title (text, required): Task title, optionally with quick-add words. project (text): List (project) name or id. Optional. due (text): Due date: 2026-11-20, 2026-11-20T15:00, today, tomorrow, a weekday (fri), in 3 days , 20/11 (day/month), or none to clear. Optional; overrides quick-add words. priority (integer): 0 none, 1 low, 2 medium, 3 high, 4 urgent, 5 do now. Optional. description (text): Notes for the task. Optional. labels (list of text): Category (label) names to attach; missing ones are created. Optional. # update_task Change a task's title, due date, priority or list. Only the fields you pass change. To change notes without losing steps use set_notes; description here replaces the whole description including steps. Changes data. id (integer, required): Task id. title (text): New title. Optional. description (text): Replaces the entire description, including steps. Prefer set_notes. Optional. due (text): Due date: 2026-11-20, 2026-11-20T15:00, today, tomorrow, a weekday (fri), in 3 days , 20/11 (day/month), or none to clear. Optional. priority (integer): 0-5. Optional. project (text): Move to this list (name or id). Optional. # complete_task Mark one or more tasks done, or reopen them with done=false. A repeating task moves to its next date and stays open, with its steps unticked. Changes data. ids (list of ids, required): Task ids. done (true/false): true marks done (default), false reopens. # move_task Move a task to a colu"},{"t":"Developer API","s":"Developers","u":"api.html","h":[["REST tools and OpenAPI","rest-tools-and-openapi"],["Tool reference","tool-reference"],["MCP","mcp"],["OAuth","oauth"],["CLI","cli"],["Vikunja API","vikunja-api"],["Building on Docket.Core","building-on-docketcore"]],"x":"Developer API Four ways to work with Docket from code, from highest to lowest level: Interface Best for Auth REST tools + OpenAPI GPT actions, automations (n8n, Zapier, Make), quick scripts Connector key (Bearer) MCP AI agents and MCP clients Connector key or OAuth docket CLI with --json Shell scripts on Windows Docket settings Vikunja REST API Anything the tools don't cover Vikunja API token The first two are served by docket-mcp --http (the Docker server runs it). They share one implementation, so they always offer the same 26 tools with the same behaviour as the Docket apps: My Day, steps, repeats and quick-add words work the same way everywhere. None of them delete. # REST tools and OpenAPI POST /api/tools/{tool} Authorization: Bearer connector key Content-Type: application/json { ...arguments } Replies: Status Body Meaning 200 { result : text } Done. The text is human-readable, with task ids as #123 400 { error : ... } Missing or wrongly typed argument 401 { error : unauthorized } Missing or wrong key 404 { error : ... } No such tool 422 { error : ... } Docket couldn't do it, e.g. No task #99 GET /openapi.json (no auth) describes every tool, its arguments, types, defaults and which ones change data ( x-openai-isConsequential ). It's generated from the code, so it's always current. Examples: KEY=your-connector-key; BASE=https://tasks.example.com curl -s $BASE/api/tools/list_tasks -H Authorization: Bearer $KEY -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{ view : today }' curl -s $BASE/api/tools/add_task -H Authorization: Bearer $KEY -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{ title : Renew passport friday !3 , project : Home , labels :[ Admin ]}' curl -s $BASE/api/tools/complete_task -H Authorization: Bearer $KEY -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -d '{ ids :[42]}' curl -s $BASE/api/tools/set_reminder -H Authorization: Bearer $KEY -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\ -d '{ id :42, times :[ tomorrow 9:00 ]}' import requests s = requests.Session() s.headers[ Authorization ] = Bearer your-connector-key r = s.post( https://tasks.example.com/api/tools/my_day , json={ suggestions : True}) print(r.json()[ result ]) # Tool reference Every tool, with its arguments and whether it changes data, is in the tool reference . It's generated from the connector's source, so it matches /openapi.json . The tool name is the {tool} in the URL and the arguments are the JSON body. Dates accept 2026-11-20 , 2026-11-20T15:00 , today , tomorrow , weekdays ( fri ), in 3 days , 20/11 (day/month) or none to clear. Lists (projects) can be given by name or id. Quick-add words in titles ( today , tomorrow , a weekday, in 3 days , 20/11 , !1 to !5 ) set the due date and priority and are removed from the title. # MCP Endpoint: POST /mcp , Streamable HTTP , stateless (no session id needed). Or run docket-mcp with no arguments for stdio. curl -s https://tasks.example.com/mcp -H Authorization: Bearer $KEY \\ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' -H 'Accept: application/json, text/event-stream' \\ -d '{ jsonrpc : 2.0 , id :1, method : tools/call , params :{ name : list_tasks , arguments :{ view : overdue }}}' Tool annotations follow the MCP spec: read tools are readOnlyHint: true ; nothing is destructiveHint: true . Errors come back as tool results with isError: true and a readable message. # OAuth For clients that need it, the connector is its own OAuth 2.1 authorization server: Endpoint GET /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource Resource metadata (RFC 9728); also linked from the 401 WWW-Authenticate header GET /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server Server metadata (RFC 8414) POST /oauth/register Dynamic client registration (RFC 7591). Redirects: localhost, or https on a DOCKET_OAUTH_REDIRECT_HOSTS host; up to 5 per client; 200 clients kept GET/POST /oauth/authorize Sign-in page: the user types the connector key once. PKCE S256 required POST /oauth/token authorization_code and refresh_token grants. Access tokens last 12 hours, refresh tokens 90 days and rotate on use Only SHA-256 hashes of tokens are stored ( oauth.json in the data folder), and a new connector key revokes them all. Ten wrong keys from one address (or 100 overall) in 15 minutes pause sign-in. Token responses are Cache-Control: no-store , and the sign-in page can't be framed. # CLI The Windows docket command prints JSON with --json on any listing: docket today --json | ConvertFrom-Json | Where-Object priority -ge 3 docket planned --json # [{ group: Today , task: {...} }, ...] docket show 42 --json Task objects look like: { id : 42, title : Send invoice , done : false, project : Work , projectId : 3, due : 2026-11-20T17:00:00+00:00 , dueText : Fri 20 Nov , priority : 3, column : To-Do , important : true, myDay : true, steps : [{ Title : Price it , Done : true }], repeat : every week , nextReminder : 2026-11-20T09:00:00+00:00 , categories : [ Admin ] } Run docket --help for every command. Exit code is 0 on success, 1 on errors, 2 on wrong usage. # Vikunja API Docket stores everything in plain Vikunja, so the Vikunja API ( /api/v1 , Swagger at /api/v1/docs on your server) sees the same data. Make a token under Settings API Tokens with only the permissions you need. How Docket maps its features onto Vikunja: Docket In Vikunja Lists and groups Projects; a group is a parent project Important is_favorite My Day The label My Day ; its description holds the day it was last reset ( Docket My Day yyyy-MM-dd ) Categories Other labels Steps A task list ( ul data-type= taskList ) at the start of the description Daily, weekly, every N days/weeks, monthly repeats Vikunja's own repeat_after / repeat_mode Weekdays, yearly, every N months/years repeats A label starting with ↻ (handled when completed through Docket) Reminders reminders Board columns Kanban buckets of the project's Kanban view Changing tasks from another tool is fine; avoid renaming the My Day label or the ↻ labels. # Building on Docket.Core src/Docket.Core (.NET 10, no UI or platform code) holds the Vikunja client a"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Help","u":"troubleshooting.html","h":[["The server stopped, or \"Starting your task server…\"","the-server-stopped-or-starting-your-task-server"],["\"This token can't delete\" (or can't share)","this-token-cant-delete-or-cant-share"],["Signed out, or \"401 unauthorized\" after a year","signed-out-or-401-unauthorized-after-a-year"],["The phone can't connect","the-phone-cant-connect"],["Reminders don't pop up","reminders-dont-pop-up"],["AI planning doesn't respond","ai-planning-doesnt-respond"],["An AI assistant doesn't see Docket","an-ai-assistant-doesnt-see-docket"],["Times are off by a few hours","times-are-off-by-a-few-hours"],["A repeating task didn't move to its next date","a-repeating-task-didnt-move-to-its-next-date"],["Where are the logs?","where-are-the-logs"],["Start fresh","start-fresh"]],"x":"Troubleshooting Find your problem below. Most fixes take a minute. If yours isn't here, ask in Discussions or report a bug . # The server stopped, or Starting your task server… On a Windows install, the task server runs on your PC. If it stops, Docket fixes it for you: The app, CLI and AI connector start it again and carry on. Starting your task server… for a few seconds is normal. Task Scheduler checks every 5 minutes and restarts it if needed. If the app says it couldn't start the server: Choose Start server and retry . Still stuck? Open Task Scheduler Task Scheduler Library Docket , right-click Vikunja server Run , and check it isn't Disabled . Look at the log in %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server\\logs for the reason (for example address already in use if another program took port 3456). Restart the PC, or run install.ps1 again. It repairs the install and keeps your tasks. On a Docker server or someone else's Vikunja, Docket can't start it from your PC; the app says it can't reach the server. Check the server is up: docker compose ps , and docker compose logs vikunja . # This token can't delete (or can't share) The app is using a token without delete or sharing rights, usually the AI connector's no-delete token, or a token made by an older installer. Make fresh tokens: $env:LOCALAPPDATA\\Programs\\Docket\\setup-tokens.ps1 Sign in when asked. It gives the Docket app a full token and the AI connector a fresh no-delete one. For a server elsewhere, add -ServerUrl https://your-server . (From a clone of the repository the script is deploy\\windows\\setup-tokens.ps1 .) If an AI assistant says it can't delete or share: that's on purpose. Do it in the Docket app. # Signed out, or 401 unauthorized after a year Tokens made by the installer last a year. When they expire the apps can't sign in. Run setup-tokens.ps1 as above (Windows), or sign in again on Android. # The phone can't connect Is Tailscale on on both the phone and the PC, signed in to the same account? Both should show as connected in the Tailscale app. Is the address right? Run tailscale serve status on the PC and use exactly the https://….ts.net address it shows. Don't add a port. Is the PC awake and signed in? The server runs while you're signed in to Windows. Open the address in the phone's browser. If Docket web or Vikunja loads there, the network is fine and it's a sign-in problem: check the username and password. Docker server: use https://your-domain and check the certificate works in a browser. More: phone access . # Reminders don't pop up On Windows , pop-ups come from the notifier docket notify --watch , not from the app window. Check it runs: Task Scheduler Docket Docket reminders . No such task? Run the installer again with -Reminders , or start it yourself with docket notify --watch . Check Windows lets it notify: Settings System Notifications, find Docket , and turn it on. Turn off Do not disturb / Focus. Run docket notify --once to show anything due now straight away. On Android , allow notifications for Docket, allow alarms and reminders if asked, and turn off battery optimisation for Docket. Open the app after adding reminders on another device, so the phone schedules them. # AI planning doesn't respond For the /ai command and the sparkle button: Turn on an AI in Settings first : choose a provider in Settings AI . Local (Ollama): Settings AI should say Local AI is running . If not, choose Start Ollama (or Install Ollama ). You need a chat model: download one there (e.g. qwen2.5:7b ) and pick it in Model . The first answer after starting can take up to a minute while the model loads. Later ones are faster. Very slow? The model may be too big for your PC. Try a smaller one, close other heavy apps. Check from PowerShell: ollama list shows installed models; ollama run qwen2.5:7b hi should answer. On Android with local AI: Ollama must listen on the network ( OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0 ) and the phone must reach the PC. See AI planning on Android . Claude, ChatGPT or Gemini: check the API key (paste it again and Save key ), that the account has API credit, and the model name. Test in Settings shows the provider's error message. # An AI assistant doesn't see Docket Where Check Claude Desktop Quit it from the tray icon (not just the window) and reopen. Check the path in its config, and that the file has no byte-order mark Claude Code claude mcp get docket , or /mcp in a session claude.ai Open https://your-domain/connector/health from a phone on mobile data. It must say status : ok and be reachable from the internet ChatGPT After a Docket upgrade, delete and re-add the connector so it sees new tools Any Docket isn't set up yet : the connector has no token. Sign in with the installer, or run docket-mcp setup More in Claude , ChatGPT and other clients . # Times are off by a few hours The server's time zone is wrong. Windows install: run install.ps1 again with -TimeZone Your/Zone . Docker: set TZ in .env and run docker compose up -d . # A repeating task didn't move to its next date Weekdays, yearly and every-N-months or -years repeats (with a ↻ category) only move forward when completed in a Docket app, the CLI or through the AI connector, not in Vikunja's web app or Docket web. Reopen it and complete it in Docket. # Where are the logs? What Where Task server (Windows) %LOCALAPPDATA%\\Docket\\server\\logs Docker services docker compose logs -f vikunja (or connector , web , caddy ) Scheduled tasks Task Scheduler Docket the task History tab # Start fresh Uninstall with -RemoveData and install again; see uninstalling . Make a backup first if there's anything you want to keep."},{"t":"Changelog","s":"Help","u":"changelog.html","h":[["0.3.0 (unreleased)","030-unreleased"],["People, sharing and teams","people-sharing-and-teams"],["Subtasks, history and icons","subtasks-history-and-icons"],["Add bar, commands and AI","add-bar-commands-and-ai"],["Views, boards and list style","views-boards-and-list-style"],["Web","web"],["Reliability","reliability"],["Also in this release","also-in-this-release"]],"x":"Changelog # 0.3.0 (unreleased) First public release. # People, sharing and teams People are accounts on your Docket server, added by email or as local people (the Windows app creates them on a local server). Assign tasks to them; assigning shares the list automatically when needed. Share a list with a person or a team (can view / can edit / can edit and share) or through a public link, with an optional password. Teams and their members are managed on the People page. # Subtasks, history and icons Subtasks are real tasks, nested to any depth, and a task can sit under several parents. Every subtask has its own star, dates, reminders and subtasks. Old checklist steps turn into subtasks with one click ( docket convert ). Task history (created by and when, last changed, completed) and row icons for notes, files, reminders, repeats, subtask progress, priority and assignees. # Add bar, commands and AI The add bar sits at the bottom. /s searches, /x marks the matches done, /m adds them to My Day, /i stars them, after a confirmation. AI planning ( /ai , or the sparkle button): describe what you need and get a nested plan to confirm. Uses Ollama on your PC (it picks an installed model) or a Claude, ChatGPT or Gemini API key, stored encrypted. # Views, boards and list style Overview dashboard, Upcoming, Open tasks, priorities everywhere, start and end dates. Every list switches between List, Table, Gantt (timeline on Android) and Board. Board columns get their own colour and icon; add as many as you like. To-Do and Done are permanent but can be renamed. Each list can have an icon (Fluent glyph or emoji) and a background: colour, gradient, accent or image. # Web docket-web : Vikunja's own web app with the Docket look in light and dark and no Vikunja branding, nothing removed. Runs at http://localhost:3457 on Windows and as the web service in Docker. # Reliability If the task server on your PC has stopped, the Windows app, the CLI and the AI connector start it again and retry before showing an error. If it still won't start, the app offers Start server and retry . The server, Docket web and reminder tasks re-run every 5 minutes as a failsafe (a running copy is left alone), and the reminders watcher keeps going through outages and restarts the server. # Also in this release Windows app (WinUI 3) with the Microsoft To Do feature set: My Day with suggestions, Important, Planned, All, Completed, lists and groups, steps, reminders, repeats, categories, files, notes, boards, undo. Android app (.NET MAUI) with the same features and Fluent design. docket CLI with --json output and Windows reminder notifications. AI connector docket-mcp : 26 tools over MCP (stdio and Streamable HTTP), REST with generated OpenAPI 3.1, built-in OAuth 2.1 (dynamic client registration, PKCE) for claude.ai and ChatGPT, docket-mcp setup for a no-delete token. Runs on Windows, Linux and macOS. Self-hosting: Windows one-click installer (no admin), Docker Compose stack with Caddy HTTPS. Releases built by GitHub Actions: Windows x64/arm64 zip, Android APK/AAB, connector binaries, container image."}];
